{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiwamu","familyName":"Fujiki","name":"Fujiki, Kiwamu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shota","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Shota","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akiko","familyName":"Takeda","name":"Takeda, Akiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Approximate Bregman proximal gradient algorithm with variable metric Armijo line search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:49:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-20T05:53:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:30:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-23T02:28:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-26T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a variant of the approximate Bregman proximal gradient (ABPG) algorithm for minimizing the sum of a smooth nonconvex function and a nonsmooth convex function. ABPG is known to converge globally to a stationary point even when the smooth part of the objective function does not have a globally Lipschitz continuous gradient, and its iterates can often be expressed in closed form. However, ABPG relies on an Armijo line search to guarantee global convergence, which can slow down its practical performance. To address this issue, we propose a variant of ABPG with a variable metric Armijo line search, which relaxes the Armijo condition. We prove that the line search terminates in finitely many steps for a general nonsmooth convex function. Under the variable metric Armijo condition, we establish global subsequential convergence of the algorithm. Moreover, assuming the Kurdyka--Łojasiewicz property, we also prove that the algorithm globally converges to a stationary point. Numerical experiments on $\\ell_p$-regularized least squares problems, nonnegative linear inverse problems and phase retrieval demonstrate that the proposed algorithm outperforms existing algorithms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-26T02:32:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lekang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lekang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Goetz","name":"Goetz, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning for Hierarchical Text Classification: The Case of Patents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-17T07:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical text classification (HTC) assigns documents to multiple levels of a pre-defined taxonomy. Automated patent subject classification represents one of the hardest HTC scenarios because of professional difficulties and extensive labels. Prior approaches only output a flat label set, which offers little insight into the reason behind predictions. Therefore, we propose Reasoning for Hierarchical Classification (RHC), a novel framework that reformulates HTC as a step-by-step reasoning task to sequentially deduce hierarchical labels. RHC trains large language models (LLMs) in two stages: a cold-start stage that aligns outputs with chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning format and a reinforcement learning (RL) stage to enhance multi-step reasoning ability. RHC demonstrates four advantages in our experiments. (1) Effectiveness: RHC surpasses previous baselines and outperforms the supervised fine-tuning counterparts by approximately 3% in accuracy and macro F1. (2) Explainability: RHC produces natural-language justifications before prediction to facilitate human inspection. (3) Scalability: RHC scales favorably with model size with larger gains compared to standard fine-tuning. (4) Applicability: Beyond patents, we further demonstrate that RHC achieves state-of-the-art performance on other widely used HTC benchmarks, which highlights its broad applicability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings. 16 pages, 10 tables, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06225","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06225","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziteng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized Multi-agent Social Simulation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T09:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:39:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent social interaction has clearly benefited from Large Language Models. However, current simulation systems still face challenges such as difficulties in scaling to diverse scenarios and poor reusability due to a lack of modular design. To address these issues, we designed and developed a modular, object-oriented framework that organically integrates various base classes through a hierarchical structure, harvesting scalability and reusability. We inherited the framework to realize common derived classes. Additionally, a memory summarization mechanism is proposed to filter and distill relevant information from raw memory data, prioritizing contextually salient events and interactions. By selecting and combining some necessary derived classes, we customized a specific simulated environment. Utilizing this simulated environment, we successfully simulated human interactions on social media, replicating real-world online social behaviors. The source code for the project will be released and evolve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06225","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donggyu","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Donggyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeok","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Hyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meeyoung","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Meeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungwon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sungwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyoon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sangyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jihee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EconCausal: A Context-Aware Economic Reasoning Benchmark for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:00:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T16:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:08:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T05:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:10:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T12:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-14T04:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-15T00:40:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Socio-economic causal effects depend heavily on their institutional and environmental contexts. The same intervention can produce different, even opposite, effects across regulatory regimes, market conditions, time periods, or populations. This poses a challenge for large language models (LLMs) in decision-support roles: can they infer the direction of a causal effect under a specified context, and revise that judgment when the context changes?\n To address this, we introduce EconCausal, a large-scale benchmark of 10,490 context-annotated causal triplets extracted from 2,595 high-quality empirical studies in top-tier economics and finance journals, constructed through a rigorous four-stage pipeline with multi-run consensus, context refinement, and multi-critic filtering.\n Across 19 models, we observe source-sign over-transfer in cross-context prediction. While top models reach ~84% accuracy in single-context prediction, mean accuracy is 74.75% overall but drops to 44.27% when source and target signs disagree. On these cases, accuracy is 11.54 percentage points (pp) lower with the signed source example than with the target input alone. Mean accuracy is below 60% under corrupted source signs. Models also struggle to predict study-reported nonsignificant effects (19.16% accuracy).\n The dataset and benchmark are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/econcausal-benchmark-6F12."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages. Revised version with updated evaluation results, robustness analyses, expert validation, and references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-15T03:09:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06640","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06640","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06640","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat M.","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Nhat M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Do Xuan","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Do Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong-Duy","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Cong-Duy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyan","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Chunyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min-Yen","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Min-Yen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luu Anh","familyName":"Tuan","name":"Tuan, Luu Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contextual Representation Flow Across Depth in State-Space and Transformer Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:46:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:24:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T04:15:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:17:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-13T12:51:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State Space Models (SSMs) have emerged as efficient, linear-time alternatives to Transformer-Based Models (TBMs) for long-context processing. However, their different mechanisms raise an important question: how does contextual representation flow across depth differ between these model families and their hybrids? We examine this question across twenty-one pretrained models, combining token-wise spectral diversity, layer-wise variation on seven datasets, and answer-location probing on two retrieval tasks. This joint analysis reveals that TBMs retain most of their token diversity at the output, whereas SSMs reach richer intermediate diversity but retain less of it on average, with late-layer compression. Comparisons with reinitialized weights reveal that these profiles depend on pretrained weights, while hybrids combine strong answer-location accessibility with architecture-dependent geometry, showing that high accessibility need not require the steadiest representation flow. Finally, we conduct theoretical case studies connecting these contrasts to mechanism-level differences: query-time token mixing in TBMs, repeated recurrent updates in SSMs, and preserved attention updates in hybrids."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06640","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Donghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younghoon","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Younghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwangsik","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Kwangsik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongryool","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jongryool","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongse","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jongse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiwan","familyName":"Maeng","name":"Maeng, Kiwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cocoon: A System Architecture for Differentially Private Training with Correlated Noises"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:56:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T18:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning (ML) models memorize and leak training data, causing serious privacy issues to data owners. Training algorithms with differential privacy (DP) have been gaining attention as a solution. However, these algorithms add noise at each training iteration and degrade accuracy, limiting their real-world adoption. To improve accuracy, a new family of approaches adds carefully designed correlated noises, so that noises cancel out each other across iterations. We performed an extensive characterization study of these new mechanisms and show they incur non-negligible overheads when the model is relatively large or uses large embedding tables compared to the hardware capacity. Motivated by the analysis, we propose Cocoon, a framework for efficient training with correlated noises. Cocoon stores and processes the large noise history across CPU, GPU, and memory extension module, introduces optimizations for sparse embedding tables, and leverages to-be-commercialized near-memory processing (NMP) devices. On a real system with an FPGA-based NMP device prototype, Cocoon improves the performance by 1.23-10.82x."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the Proceedings of 20th USENIX Symposium on Operating Systems Design and Implementation (OSDI'26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06348","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06348","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06348","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Quigg","name":"Quigg, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perspectives and Questions: Toward an Expansive Agenda for Particle Physics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T02:02:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T00:17:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Global celebration greeted the 2012 discovery at CERN's Large Hadron Collider of a particle that matches the textbook description of the Higgs boson. That achievement validated a remarkable chain of theoretical reasoning that combined the prescriptive notion of electroweak gauge symmetry with a simple, but \\emph{ad hoc}, embodiment of spontaneous symmetry breaking. It was made possible by generational triumphs of accelerator art and experimental technique, and by human resourcefulness and collaboration on a global scale, all sustained by the enlightened support of many governments and institutions.\n Some imagine that, once the keystone of the standard model of particle physics has been set, our subject is over. Others worry that we may be at an impasse because no comparable wonders have appeared, leaving us without well-defined clues to a more complete paradigm. I am neither so readily satisfied nor so {easily} discouraged: we have so much more to learn! This essay surveys many questions that, taken together, constitute an inspiring array of opportunities to enhance our understanding of the physical world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 1 figure. One typo corrected, one reference added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06348","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:13Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-14T02:17:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raaghav","familyName":"Ravishankar","name":"Ravishankar, Raaghav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Kulkarni","name":"Kulkarni, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitin H","familyName":"Vaidya","name":"Vaidya, Nitin H","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asynchronous Checkpoint for Eventually Consistent Databases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:29:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-16T19:50:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-35251-4_38","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We focus on the problem of checkpointing (or taking a snapshot) in fully replicated eventually consistent distributed databases. In particular, we consider the problem of taking Distributed Transaction-Consistent Snapshots (DTCS). A typical example of such a system is a replicated main-memory database that provides strong eventual consistency. This problem is important and challenging for several reasons: (1) eventual consistency often creates anomalies that the users do not anticipate. Hence, frequent snapshots that can be used to ascertain desired invariants are highly beneficial in their maintenance, and (2) traditional distributed snapshot algorithms lead to significant overhead and/or inconsistencies such as storing dirty writes of incomplete transactions.\n A key benefit of DTCS is that it summarizes the computation by a sequence of snapshots that are strongly consistent even though the underlying computation is only weakly consistent. In essence, when anomalies arise in an eventually consistent system, DTCS enables one to concentrate solely on the snapshots surrounding the time point of the anomaly.\n By showing that traditional distributed snapshots lead to inconsistencies and/or excessive overhead, we define the notion of size-minimal DTCS for fully replicated databases. We present MuFASA, an algorithm for a size-minimal DTCS with minimal checkpointing overhead (only O(n) new messages and the addition of a single counter for existing messages). MuFASA also provides a significant benefit over existing checkpointing algorithms for distributed systems and replicated main-memory databases by being a fully asynchronous protocol."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ghazi Sarwat","familyName":"Syed","name":"Syed, Ghazi Sarwat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Brückerhoff-Plückelmann","name":"Brückerhoff-Plückelmann, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jelle","familyName":"Dijkstra","name":"Dijkstra, Jelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfram H. P","familyName":"Pernice","name":"Pernice, Wolfram H. P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abu","familyName":"Sebastian","name":"Sebastian, Abu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fully Parallel Multi-Agent Photonic Optimizer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T13:57:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimization problems are central to many important cross-disciplinary applications.In their conventional implementations, the sequential nature of operations imposes strict limitations on the computational efficiency. Here, we discuss how analog optical computing can overcome this fundamental bottleneck. We propose a photonic optimizer unit, together with supporting algorithms that uses in memory computation within a nature inspired, multi agent cooperative framework. The system performs a sequence of reconfigurable parallel matrix vector operations, enabled by the high bandwidth and multiplexing capabilities inherent to photonic circuits. This approach provides a pathway toward fast paced and high quality solutions for difficult optimization and search problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nian","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongzheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhongzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikun","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Allmendinger","name":"Allmendinger, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCCE: A Framework for Multi-LLM Collaborative Search in Discrete Spaces with Similarity-Filtered Preference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T07:42:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T00:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-objective discrete optimization problems, such as molecular design, pose significant challenges due to their vast and unstructured combinatorial spaces. Traditional evolutionary algorithms often get trapped in local optima, while expert knowledge can provide crucial guidance for accelerating convergence. Large language models (LLMs) offer powerful priors and reasoning ability, making them natural optimizers when expert knowledge matters. However, closed-source LLMs, though strong in exploration, cannot update their parameters and thus cannot internalize experience. Conversely, smaller open models can be continually fine-tuned but lack broad knowledge and reasoning strength. We introduce Multi-LLM Collaborative Co-evolution (MCCE), a hybrid framework that unites a frozen closed-source LLM with a lightweight trainable model. The system maintains a trajectory memory of past search processes; the small model is progressively refined via reinforcement learning, with the two models jointly supporting and complementing each other in global exploration. Unlike model distillation, this process enhances the capabilities of both models through mutual inspiration. Experiments on multi-objective drug design benchmarks show that MCCE achieves state-of-the-art Pareto front quality and consistently outperforms baselines. These results highlight a new paradigm for enabling continual evolution in hybrid LLM systems, combining knowledge-driven exploration with experience-driven learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Weidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaogeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaogeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-Chun","familyName":"Chiu","name":"Chiu, Hung-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xusheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhi","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yinzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Harness is Not Secure: Benchmarking Real-world Threat of Command Line Interface Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:18:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-21T05:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:21:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Command-line interface (CLI) agents powered by large language models (LLMs) can interpret natural-language requests, plan multi-step tasks, execute shell commands, and modify files and system state. As these agents are increasingly used for operating-system (OS) workflows, it is important to evaluate whether they can be misused to carry out security-relevant operations. Existing benchmarks often lack an attacker-knowledge model grounded in tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs), provide limited coverage of end-to-end kill chains, rely on simplified single-host environments, or use LLM-as-a-judge for success evaluation. We introduce AdvCLI, an MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK-aligned benchmark for evaluating OS-level misuse risks of CLI agents in a controlled multi-host sandbox. AdvCLI contains 140 tasks: 40 direct malicious requests, 74 TTP-based tasks, and 26 end-to-end kill chains. Each task is paired with deterministic hard-coded verification protocols that check whether the requested OS-level effect is realized. We evaluate seven CLI agents and products built on nine foundation models, including ReAct, OpenClaw, OpenAI Agent SDK, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor CLI, and Cursor IDE. Results show that current CLI agents frequently proceed beyond refusal and can complete a non-negligible fraction of malicious OS-level tasks, especially when requests include TTP-style attacker knowledge. AdvCLI provides a reproducible testbed for evaluating these risks and for developing stronger safety mechanisms for tool-using CLI agents in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:11Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-24T02:23:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akitoshi","familyName":"Kawamura","name":"Kawamura, Akitoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Kobayashi","name":"Kobayashi, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Computer-Assisted Proof of the Optimal Density Bound for Pinwheel Covering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:14:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:36:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:16:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T04:58:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-20T00:25:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the covering version of the pinwheel scheduling problem, a daily task must be assigned to agents under the constraint that agent $i$ can perform the task at most once in any $a_i$-day interval. In this paper, we determine the optimal constant $α^* = 1.264\\ldots$ such that every instance with $\\sum_{i} 1 / a_i \\ge α^*$ is schedulable. This resolves an open problem posed by Kawamura and Soejima (2020). Our proof combines Kawamura's (2026) techniques for the packing version with new mathematical insights to reduce the analysis to a finite set of instances, which are then verified through an exhaustive computer-aided search that draws on ideas from Gąsieniec, Smith, and Wild (2022). The same result was obtained independently by Mishra (2026)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A conference version will appear in ESA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-20T02:18:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yatao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yatao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konrad","familyName":"Schindler","name":"Schindler, Konrad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Raubal","name":"Raubal, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Generative Model for Human Mobility Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:22:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T20:12:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:46:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T18:38:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:06:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-09T17:00:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-11T01:12:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and modeling human mobility is central to challenges in transport planning, sustainable urban design, and public health. Despite decades of effort, simulating individual mobility remains challenging because of its complex, context-dependent, and exploratory nature. Here, building on the activity-based view of daily mobility, we propose MobilityGen, a diffusion-based generative framework for simulating multi-attribute activity-travel sequences over days to weeks at large spatial scales. By linking behavioral attributes with environmental context, MobilityGen reproduces key patterns such as scaling laws for location visits, activity time allocation, and the coupled evolution of travel mode and destination choices. It reflects spatio-temporal variability and generates diverse and plausible mobility patterns consistent with the built environment. Beyond standard validation, MobilityGen enables analyses that have been difficult with earlier models, including how access to urban space varies across travel modes and how co-presence dynamics shape social exposure and segregation. Together, these results support an integrated, data-driven basis for fine-grained studies of human mobility behavior and its societal implications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at TRC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Illing","name":"Illing, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Althoff","name":"Althoff, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SanDRA: Safe Large-Language-Model-Based Decision Making for Automated Vehicles Using Reachability Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-11T02:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:16:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been widely applied to knowledge-driven decision-making for automated vehicles due to their strong generalization and reasoning capabilities. However, the safety of the resulting decisions cannot be ensured due to possible hallucinations and the lack of integrated vehicle dynamics. To address this issue, we propose SanDRA, the first safe large-language-model-based decision making framework for automated vehicles using reachability analysis. Our approach starts with a comprehensive description of the driving scenario to prompt LLMs to generate and rank feasible driving actions. These actions are translated into temporal logic formulas that incorporate formalized traffic rules, and are subsequently integrated into reachability analysis to eliminate unsafe actions. We validate our approach in both open-loop and closed-loop driving environments using off-the-shelf and finetuned LLMs, showing that it can provide provably safe and, where possible, legally compliant driving actions, even under high-density traffic conditions. To ensure transparency and facilitate future research, all code and experimental setups are publicly available at github.com/CommonRoad/SanDRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"@2026 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T03:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fangxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiji","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Shiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constant Weighted Maximin Share Approximations for Chores"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T02:24:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:18:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-07T22:41:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:10:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the fair allocation of indivisible chores among agents with asymmetric weights. Among the various fairness notions, weighted maximin share (WMMS) stands out as particularly compelling. Despite its appeal, the existence of a constant-factor approximation for WMMS has remained an important open problem in weighted fair division [Aziz et al., 2022, Suksompong, 2025]. Prior to our work, the best known approximation ratio was $O(\\log n)$, where $n$ is the number of agents. In this paper, we make significant progress by presenting the first constant-factor approximation algorithm for WMMS. Our main contributions are as follows:\n [1] We design the first algorithm that guarantees a 12-approximate WMMS allocation, substantially improving upon the previous $O(\\log n)$ upper bound. Our approach introduces a novel analytical framework based on canonical instance reductions, agent delegation, and proxy cost functions to effectively bound agents' costs. Additionally, we provide a polynomial-time implementation for any approximate WMMS algorithm, incurring a factor of 2 loss in the approximation ratio.\n [2] We present an improved worst-case lower bound, showing that no algorithm can achieve better than 2-approximate WMMS, thereby strengthening the previous best lower bound of 1.366. We further construct a general hard instance, which provides lower bounds for an arbitrary number of agents.\n [3] Beyond worst-case bounds, we precisely characterize the optimal approximation ratio curve for every possible weight distribution in the two-agent case. Notably, our results imply that a WMMS allocation may not exist for any two agents with different weights, in sharp contrast to the symmetric case where an MMS allocation always exists."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:02:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Ghugare","name":"Ghugare, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger Creus","familyName":"Castanyer","name":"Castanyer, Roger Creus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catherine","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Catherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathryn","familyName":"Wantlin","name":"Wantlin, Kathryn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Schofield","name":"Schofield, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Narasimhan","name":"Narasimhan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Eysenbach","name":"Eysenbach, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BuilderBench: The Building Blocks of Intelligent Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T02:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T13:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's AI models learn primarily through mimicry and refining, so it is not surprising that they struggle to solve problems beyond the limits set by existing data. To solve novel problems, agents should acquire skills by exploring and learning through experience. Finding a scalable learning mechanism for developing agents that learn through interaction remains a major open problem. In this work, we introduce BuilderBench, a benchmark to accelerate research into agent training that centers open-ended exploration. BuilderBench requires agents to learn how to build any structure using blocks. BuilderBench is equipped with (1) a simulator of a robot interacting with various physical blocks, and (2) a task-suite with over 50 diverse target structures that are carefully curated to test an understanding of physics, mathematics, and long-horizon planning. Agents are provided with a target structure at the start, and can interact with the environment for multiple episodes to experiment and learn various skills for building the structure. Solving these tasks requires \\emph{embodied reasoning} in a way that is not reflected in words but rather in actions, experimenting with different strategies and piecing them together. Our experiments with multiple state-of-the-art frontier language model based agents and tabula rasa reinforcement learning algorithms show that these agents cannot solve any of the non-trivial tasks in the BuilderBench. Our analysis throws light on the lack of exploration abilities in these models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Blogpost: https://rajghugare19.github.io/builderbench and Code: https://github.com/rajghugare19/builderbench"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gilles","familyName":"Barthe","name":"Barthe, Gilles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minbo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Minbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jam Kabeer Ali","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Jam Kabeer Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthijs","familyName":"Muis","name":"Muis, Matthijs","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Renison","name":"Renison, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiya","familyName":"Sakabe","name":"Sakabe, Keiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Walter","name":"Walter, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingte","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yingte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Complete Relational Logic for Infinite-Dimensional Quantum Programs with Unbounded Assertions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:19:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T16:41:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:01:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present sound and complete relational program logics for infinite-dimensional quantum and classical-quantum programs. The logics model assertions as self-adjoint unbounded linear relations, which simultaneously support quantitative and qualitative reasoning. Our main theoretical results include new convergence theorems and infinite-dimensional duality theorems for infinite-dimensional quantum states, which we use to establish completeness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"65 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables. Full version of the paper accepted to LICS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:11:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsheng","familyName":"Ba","name":"Ba, Jinsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sverrir","familyName":"Thorgeirsson","name":"Thorgeirsson, Sverrir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code Semantic Zooming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T09:42:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T19:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T00:02:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have introduced a new paradigm for software development, where source code is generated from natural language prompts. While this paradigm significantly boosts development productivity, building complex, real-world software systems remains challenging because natural language offers limited control over the code generation process. Inspired by the historical evolution of programming languages toward higher levels of abstraction, we advocate for a high-level abstraction language that gives developers greater control over LLM-assisted code writing. To this end, we propose Code Semantic Zooming (CodeZoom), a novel approach based on pseudocode that allows developers to iteratively explore, understand, and refine code across multiple layers of semantic abstraction. In a within-subjects user study (n=26), our method matches a state-of-the-art coding agent, Claude Code, on usability while producing a large effect on code comprehension: over 90% of participants reported feeling more in control of design decisions when using CodeZoom compared to using Claude Code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-02T02:11:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancheng","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Tiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are LLMs Reliable Rankers? Rank Manipulation via Two-Stage Token Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:40:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-28T18:43:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T01:13:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as rerankers in information retrieval, yet their ranking behavior can be steered by small, natural-sounding prompts. To expose this vulnerability, we present Rank Anything First (RAF), a two-stage token optimization method that crafts concise textual perturbations to consistently promote a target item in LLM-generated rankings while remaining hard to detect. Stage 1 uses Greedy Coordinate Gradient to shortlist candidate tokens at the current position by combining the gradient of the rank-target with a readability score; Stage 2 evaluates those candidates under exact ranking and readability losses using an entropy-based dynamic weighting scheme, and selects a token via temperature-controlled sampling. RAF generates ranking-promoting prompts token-by-token, guided by dual objectives: maximizing ranking effectiveness and preserving linguistic naturalness. Experiments across multiple LLMs show that RAF significantly boosts the rank of target items using naturalistic language, with greater robustness than existing methods in both promoting target items and maintaining naturalness. These findings underscore a critical security implication: LLM-based reranking is inherently susceptible to adversarial manipulation, raising new challenges for the trustworthiness and robustness of modern retrieval systems. Our code is available at: https://github.com/glad-lab/RAF."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06659","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06659","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06659","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shouzhen","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Shouzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libor","familyName":"Caha","name":"Caha, Libor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shin Ho","familyName":"Choe","name":"Choe, Shin Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksander","familyName":"Kubica","name":"Kubica, Aleksander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Layer codes as partially self-correcting quantum memories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:17:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T05:16:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:29:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/x4z2-f4gg","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate layer codes, a family of three-dimensional stabilizer codes that can achieve optimal scaling of code parameters and a polynomial energy barrier, as candidates for self-correcting quantum memories. First, we introduce two decoding algorithms for layer codes with provable guarantees for local stochastic and adversarial noise, respectively. We then prove that layer codes constitute partially self-correcting quantum memories which outperform previously analyzed models such as the cubic code and the welded solid code. Notably, we argue that partial self-correction without the requirement of efficient decoding is more common than expected, as it arises solely from a diverging energy barrier. This draws a sharp distinction between partially self-correcting systems and partially self-correcting memories. Another novel aspect of our work is an analysis of layer codes constructed from random Calderbank-Shor-Steane codes. We show that these random layer codes have optimal scaling (up to logarithmic corrections) of code parameters and a polynomial energy barrier. Finally, we present numerical studies of their memory times and report behavior consistent with partial self-correction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages, 17 figures, updated to match publication version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06659","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Autenrieb","name":"Autenrieb, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Spiller","name":"Spiller, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decentralized CBF-based Safety Filters for Collision Avoidance of Cooperative Missile Systems with Input Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:12:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T12:40:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:47:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T15:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T02:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-18T11:41:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a decentralized safety filter for collision avoidance in multi-agent aerospace interception scenarios. The approach leverages robust control barrier functions (RCBFs) to guarantee forward invariance of safe sets under bounded inputs and high-relative-degree dynamics. Each effector executes its nominal cooperative guidance command, while a local quadratic program (QP) modifies the input only when necessary. Event-triggered activation based on range and zero-effort miss (ZEM) criteria ensures scalability by restricting active constraints to relevant neighbors. To ensure feasibility under multiple simultaneously active constraints, a slack-variable relaxation scheme is introduced that prioritizes critical agents in a Pareto-optimal manner. Simulation results in many-on-many interception scenarios demonstrate that the proposed framework maintains collision-free operation with minimal deviation from nominal guidance, providing a computationally efficient and scalable solution for safety-critical multi-agent aerospace systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 5 figures, accepted for presentation at the 2026 American Control Conference (ACC 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:17:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Hinsche","name":"Hinsche, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongbo","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Zongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"van Dordrecht","name":"van Dordrecht, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Eisert","name":"Eisert, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jop","familyName":"Briët","name":"Briët, Jop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Helsen","name":"Helsen, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Clifford testing: algorithms and lower bounds"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:02:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:33:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3798129.3800801","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the problem of Clifford testing, which asks whether a black-box $n$-qubit unitary is a Clifford unitary or at least $\\varepsilon$-far from every Clifford unitary. We give the first 4-query Clifford tester, which decides this problem with probability $\\mathrm{poly}(\\varepsilon)$. This contrasts with the minimum of 6 copies required for the closely-related task of stabilizer testing. We show that our tester is tolerant, by adapting techniques from tolerant stabilizer testing to our setting. In doing so, we settle in the positive a conjecture of Bu, Gu and Jaffe, by proving a polynomial inverse theorem for a non-commutative Gowers 3-uniformity norm. We also consider the restricted setting of single-copy access, where we give an $O(n)$-query Clifford tester that requires no auxiliary memory qubits or adaptivity. We complement this with a lower bound, proving that any such, potentially adaptive, single-copy algorithm needs at least $Ω(n^{1/4})$ queries. To obtain our results, we leverage the structure of the commutant of the Clifford group, obtaining several technical statements that may be of independent interest."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages. Comments welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:07:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Shahriar","name":"Shahriar, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nafiu","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Md Nafiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Sadif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farig","familyName":"Sadeque","name":"Sadeque, Farig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rizwan","familyName":"Parvez","name":"Parvez, Md Rizwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey on Agentic Security: Applications, Threats and Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-20T13:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T21:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based agents are now used throughout cybersecurity. While these agents facilitate powerful and autonomous security applications, their autonomy opens up new attack surfaces, and the security community is actively building defenses to secure them. Yet the literature on this subject has grown quickly and unevenly. Existing surveys treat applications, threats, and defenses in isolation, leaving no unified account of how an agent's capabilities, vulnerabilities, and countermeasures interconnect. In this work we present the first holistic survey of the agentic security landscape, structuring the field around the fundamental pillars of Applications, Threats and Defenses. We provide a comprehensive taxonomy of over 260 papers, explaining how agents are used in downstream cybersecurity applications, inherent threats to agentic systems, and countermeasures designed to protect them. In addition, we provide detailed pillar-specific and cross-cutting analyses that show the security-lifecycle coverage of agentic applications, comparison between red-teaming and blue-teaming agents, and the adversarial use of red-teaming applications. On the threat side, we analyze the entry points and agent-loop stages that attacks target, their specificity to the agentic setting, and the threat models they assume. On the defense side, we analyze the prevailing defense strategies, their cost and security trade-offs, and where in the agent lifecycle they are deployed. We further map which defenses cover which attack classes and chart trends in agent architecture, backbone model usage, data modality coverage, and the growth of attack and defense research over time. Taken together, these findings indicate that agentic systems are structurally fragile by default and that securing them will require defenses that span the full agent lifecycle rather than single-layer fixes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:27Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andersen","familyName":"Ang","name":"Ang, Andersen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Stein","name":"Stein, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Network Pricing for Oblivious Users under Projected Decision-Dependent Distributions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.2.1; F.2.1; F.2.2; G.0; H.5.3; I.2.0; I.6.0; J.1; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"65F50, 90B06, 90C06, 90C15, 90C20, 90C26, 90C30, 91B70","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T12:46:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:53:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-23T19:24:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:44:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-27T14:36:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-28T01:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-30T18:41:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:48:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient large-scale network allocation requires data-driven pricing mechanisms that internalize stochastic, nonlinear user behavior. We move beyond the classic fully strategic agents to study oblivious users (agents with bounded rationality and imperfect information). Rather than assuming an infinite horizon, our regime acknowledges that real-world flows are too transient to equilibrate among users. We introduce a novel Optimal Network Pricing (ONP) problem for such users, which induces Performativity: a Decision-Dependent environment where pricing decisions endogenously shift the flow distribution. Without a closed-form distribution, the platform must learn optimal prices from sampled responses.\n This setting introduces a new challenge: capacity boundaries and projection operators make the optimization landscape nonsmooth, invalidating gradient-based methods. We show that a widely adopted optimality concept Performative Stability (PS) fails in ONP, collapsing to a trivial solution. We then define a new optimality concept, the Projected Performative Optimum (ΠPO) for the unique global optimum. Targeting ΠPO is algorithmically hard given the performative nonsmooth Jacobian, so we propose a novel framework combining Sample Average Approximation with Trust-Region Sequential Quadratic Programming, explicitly handling the capacity boundaries, with theoretical guarantees on probabilistic convexity, sample complexity, and computational complexity. Experiments show that our ΠPO solver significantly outperforms PS-seeking heuristics and a proposed baseline (improving social welfare by 81\\% on GEANT), highlighting that properly handling capacity boundaries unlocks substantial gains in social welfare. More broadly, this work advances intelligent systems that learn under performative, capacity-constrained feedback, a core challenge in real-world AI applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:13Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marlon","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Marlon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Finkeldei","name":"Finkeldei, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanna","familyName":"Krasowski","name":"Krasowski, Hanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Arcak","name":"Arcak, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Althoff","name":"Althoff, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Falsification-driven reinforcement learning for maritime motion planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:56:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T15:03:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T01:13:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.oceaneng.2026.125579","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compliance with maritime traffic rules is essential for the safe operation of autonomous vessels, yet training reinforcement learning (RL) agents to adhere to them is challenging. The behavior of RL agents is shaped by the training scenarios they encounter, but creating scenarios that capture the complexity of maritime navigation is non-trivial, and real-world data alone is insufficient. To address this, we propose a falsification-driven RL approach that generates adversarial training scenarios in which the vessel under test violates maritime traffic rules, which are expressed as signal temporal logic specifications. Our experiments on open-sea navigation with two vessels demonstrate that the proposed approach provides more relevant training scenarios and achieves more consistent rule compliance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 9 figures. Code available at https://fdrl-maritime.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06843","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06843","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanyun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:10:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:58:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive capabilities across diverse application domains. Recent work has explored Multi-LLM Agent Debate (MAD) as a way to enhance performance by enabling multiple LLMs to discuss and refine responses iteratively. Nevertheless, existing MAD methods predominantly focus on utilizing external structures, such as debate graphs, using LLM-as-a-Judge, while neglecting the application of self signals, such as token logits and attention, that arise during generation. This omission leads to redundant computation and potential performance degradation. In this paper, we shift the focus to the self signals of multi-LLM debate and introduce a Self-Signals Driven Multi-LLM Debate (SID), which leverages two types of self-signals: model-level confidence and token-level semantic focus, to adaptively guide the debate process. Our approach enables high-confidence agents to exit early at the model level and compress the redundant debate contents based on the attention mechanism. We evaluate our method on various LLMs and Multimodal LLMs across multiple challenging benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate that our method not only outperforms existing MAD techniques in accuracy but also reduces token consumption, highlighting the effectiveness of utilizing self signals in enhancing both the performance and efficiency of multi-agent debate systems. Our code will be available at~\\href{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}{\\texttt{https://github.com/xuhang2019/SID}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06843","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgenii","familyName":"Opryshko","name":"Opryshko, Evgenii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwei","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Junwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claas","familyName":"Voelcker","name":"Voelcker, Claas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Gilitschenski","name":"Gilitschenski, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Graph Search for Goal-Conditioned Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:20:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T22:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:14:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Offline goal-conditioned reinforcement learning (GCRL) often struggles with long-horizon tasks, where errors in value estimation accumulate and produce unreliable policies. It is typically assumed that effective long-term planning is infeasible without specialized training. In contrast, our work demonstrates that existing GCRL policies can complete long-horizon tasks when combined with a lightweight, training-free planning wrapper. We find that standard goal-conditioned value functions encode locally consistent geometric structure sufficient for planning. Our approach, Test-Time Graph Search (TTGS), constructs a graph over the offline dataset and employs an adaptive subgoal selection strategy. To address unreliable value estimates during shortest-path search, we propose a novel mechanism that softly penalizes long-distance transitions. Our method incurs negligible computational overhead and requires no additional supervision or parameter updates. On the OGBench benchmark, TTGS significantly boosts success rates across multiple base learners and tasks, with primary gains on challenging long-horizon locomotion tasks where some success rates are improved from near-zero to over 90\\%, often matching or outperforming methods that require complex auxiliary training. Code and videos can be found at https://ktolnos.github.io/ttgs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06672","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06672","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udbhav","familyName":"Bamba","name":"Bamba, Udbhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XRPO: Pushing the limits of GRPO with Targeted Exploration and Exploitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:19:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T01:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:20:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning algorithms such as GRPO have driven recent advances in large language model (LLM) reasoning. While scaling the number of rollouts stabilizes training, existing approaches suffer from limited exploration on challenging prompts and leave informative feedback signals underexploited, due to context-independent rollout allocation across prompts (e.g., generating 16 rollouts per prompt) and relying heavily on sparse rewards. This paper presents XRPO(eXplore - eXploit GRPO), a unified framework that recasts policy optimization through the principled lens of rollout exploration-exploitation. To enhance exploration, XRPO introduces a mathematically grounded rollout allocator that adaptively prioritizes prompts with higher potential for uncertainty reduction. It further addresses stagnation on zero-reward prompts through an in-context seeding strategy that injects curated exemplars, steering the model into more difficult reasoning trajectories. To strengthen exploitation, XRPO develops a group-relative, novelty-aware advantage sharpening mechanism that leverages sequence likelihoods to amplify low-probability yet correct responses, thereby extending the policy's reach beyond sparse rewards. Experiments across diverse math and coding benchmarks on both reasoning and non-reasoning models demonstrate that XRPO outperforms existing advances (e.g., GRPO and GSPO) up to 4% pass@1 and 6% cons@32, while accelerating training convergence by up to 2.7X."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06672","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaleb Ben","familyName":"Naveed","name":"Naveed, Kaleb Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devansh R.","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Devansh R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitra","familyName":"Panagou","name":"Panagou, Dimitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Formal gatekeeper Framework for Safe Dual Control with Active Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:12:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T22:33:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Planning safe trajectories under model uncertainty is a fundamental challenge. Robust planning ensures safety by considering worst-case realizations, yet ignores uncertainty reduction and leads to overly conservative behavior. Actively reducing uncertainty on-the-fly during a nominal mission defines the dual control problem. Most approaches address this by adding a weighted exploration term to the cost, tuned to trade off the nominal objective and uncertainty reduction, but without formal consideration of when exploration is beneficial. Moreover, safety is enforced in some methods but not in others. We propose a framework that integrates robust planning with active exploration under formal guarantees as follows: The key innovation and contribution is that exploration is pursued only when it provides a verifiable improvement without compromising safety. To achieve this, we utilize our earlier work on gatekeeper as an architecture for safety verification, and extend it so that it generates both safe and informative trajectories that reduce uncertainty and the cost of the mission, or keep it within a user-defined budget. The methodology is evaluated via simulation case studies on the online dual control of a quadrotor under parametric uncertainty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at American Control Conference (ACC) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:17Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Kreitner","name":"Kreitner, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Hager","name":"Hager, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Mengedoht","name":"Mengedoht, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios","familyName":"Kaissis","name":"Kaissis, Georgios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Rueckert","name":"Rueckert, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin J.","familyName":"Menten","name":"Menten, Martin J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient numeracy in language models through single-token number embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:48:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-20T14:19:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To drive progress in science and engineering, large language models (LLMs) must be able to process large amounts of numerical data and solve long calculations efficiently. This is currently only possible through the use of external tools or extensive reasoning chains, either weakening the numerical representations of LLMs or limiting the length of problems they can solve. We show that frontier LLMs require excessive amounts of reasoning tokens to solve even basic calculations, which is exacerbated by their tokenization strategies that split single numbers into multiple tokens. This motivates the need for efficient and effective single-token number encodings. We introduce a set of desiderata for such encodings and show that existing approaches fail to fulfill them. To address these shortcomings, we propose BitTokens, a novel encoding strategy that represents any number as a single token using its IEEE 754 binary floating-point representation. Through extensive experiments we show that our BitTokens allow even small language models to learn algorithms that solve basic arithmetic operations nearly perfectly. This newly gained efficiency could expand the length and complexity of problems language models can solve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junki","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Junki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Kakizaki","name":"Kakizaki, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiki","familyName":"Miyagawa","name":"Miyagawa, Taiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentially Private Synthetic Text Generation for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:15:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:29:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-12T15:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) by grounding them in external knowledge. However, its application in sensitive domains is limited by privacy risks. Existing private RAG methods typically rely on query-time differential privacy (DP), which requires repeated noise injection and leads to accumulated privacy loss. To address this issue, we propose DP-SynRAG, a framework that uses LLMs to generate differentially private synthetic RAG databases. Unlike prior methods, the synthetic text can be reused once created, thereby avoiding repeated noise injection and additional privacy costs. To preserve essential information for downstream RAG tasks, DP-SynRAG extends private prediction, which instructs LLMs to generate text that mimics subsampled database records in a DP manner. Experiments show that DP-SynRAG achieves superior performance to the state-of-the-art private RAG systems while maintaining a fixed privacy budget, offering a scalable solution for privacy-preserving RAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firoj","familyName":"Alam","name":"Alam, Firoj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Ezzat","familyName":"Shahroor","name":"Shahroor, Ali Ezzat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Arid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Arid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zien Sheikh","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Zien Sheikh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hunzalah Hassan","familyName":"Bhatti","name":"Bhatti, Hunzalah Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Bayan","familyName":"Kmainasi","name":"Kmainasi, Mohamed Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shammur Absar","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Shammur Absar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basel","familyName":"Mousi","name":"Mousi, Basel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahim","familyName":"Dalvi","name":"Dalvi, Fahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadir","familyName":"Durrani","name":"Durrani, Nadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasa","familyName":"Milic-Frayling","name":"Milic-Frayling, Natasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OASIS: A Multilingual and Multimodal Dataset for Culturally Grounded Spoken Visual QA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:37:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-09T06:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:32:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T05:22:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:35:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale multimodal models achieve strong results on tasks like Visual Question Answering (VQA), but they are often limited when queries require cultural and visual information, everyday knowledge, particularly in low-resource and underrepresented languages. We introduce OASIS, a large-scale culturally grounded multimodal QA dataset covering images, text, and speech. OASIS is built with EverydayMMQA, a scalable semi-automatic framework for creating localized spoken and visual QA resources, supported by multi-stage human-in-the-loop validation. OASIS contains approximately 0.92M real images and 14.8M QA pairs, including 3.7M spoken questions, with 383 hours of human-recorded speech, and 20K hours of voice-cloned speech, from 42 speakers. It supports four input settings: text-only, speech-only, text+image, and speech+image. The dataset focuses on English and Arabic varieties across 18 countries, covering Modern Standard Arabic (MSA) as well as dialectal Arabic. It is designed to evaluate models beyond object recognition, targeting pragmatic, commonsense, and culturally grounded reasoning in real-world scenarios. We benchmark four closed-source models, three open-source models, and one fine-tuned model on OASIS. The framework and dataset will be made publicly available to the community. https://huggingface.co/datasets/QCRI/OASIS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Multimodal Foundation Models, Large Language Models, Native, Multilingual, Language Diversity, Contextual Understanding, Culturally Informed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonggeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jonggeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haesung","familyName":"Pyun","name":"Pyun, Haesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Don't Adapt Small Language Models for Tools; Adapt Tool Schemas to the Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T09:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-19T15:11:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:15:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs) enable scalable tool-augmented multi-agent systems where multiple SLMs handle subtasks orchestrated by a powerful coordinator. However, they struggle with tool-use tasks, particularly in selecting appropriate tools and identifying correct parameters. A common failure mode is \\textit{schema misalignment}: models hallucinate plausible tool names that are absent from the provided tool schema, due to different naming conventions internalized during pretraining. Rather than training models to adapt to unfamiliar schemas, we propose adapting schemas to align with models' pretrained knowledge. We introduce \\textbf{PA-Tool} (Pretraining-Aligned Tool Schema Generation), a training-free method that leverages peakedness, a signal used in contamination detection that indicates pretraining familiarity, to rename tool components. By generating multiple candidates and selecting the candidate with the highest peakedness, PA-Tool identifies pretraining-aligned naming patterns. Experiments on MetaTool and RoTBench show improvements of up to 17\\%, with schema misalignment errors reduced by 80\\%. PA-Tool enables small models to substantially improve tool-use accuracy without retraining, showing that schema-level interventions can unlock the tool-use potential of resource-efficient models. Our code is available at https://github.com/holi-lab/PA-Tool."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wensong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Haocong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lutao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are We Using the Right Benchmark: An Evaluation Framework for Visual Token Compression Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T06:35:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:20:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts to accelerate inference in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have largely focused on visual token compression. The effectiveness of these methods is commonly evaluated by measuring the accuracy drop on existing MLLM benchmarks before and after compression. However, these benchmarks are originally designed to assess general perception and reasoning abilities, rather than the specific challenges posed by visual token compression, leading to a fundamental task mismatch. In this work, we uncover a counterintuitive yet consistent phenomenon: simple image downsampling outperforms many advanced visual token compression methods across multiple widely used benchmarks. Through a comprehensive empirical study spanning eight popular benchmarks and multiple state-of-the-art compression techniques, we show that (i) current benchmarks contain substantial noise (task-irrelevant samples) for evaluating visual token compression, and (ii) downsampling can act as an effective data filter that distinguishes between simple and difficult samples with respect to compression sensitivity. Motivated by these findings, we propose VTC-Bench, an evaluation framework that explicitly leverages downsampling as a discriminator to denoise existing benchmarks, enabling a fairer and more meaningful additional assessment of visual token compression methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL2026 Main. Code: https://github.com/Chenfei-Liao/VTC-Bench; Project Page: https://chenfei-liao.github.io/VTC-Bench-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfei","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengdi","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Fengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriEquivBench: An Equivalence Score for Ground-Truth-Free Evaluation of Formally Verifiable Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:19:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T09:53:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:47:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-18T14:30:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:45:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Formal verification is the next frontier for ensuring the correctness of code generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). While methods that co-generate code and formal specifications in formal languages, like Dafny, can, in principle, prove alignment with user intent, progress is bottlenecked by specification quality evaluation. Current benchmarks rely on matching against ground-truth specifications, a manual and expertise-intensive process that has limited existing datasets to a few hundred simple problems and also suffers from a reliability issue. To address this, we introduce VeriEquivBench, a new benchmark with $2,389$ complex algorithmic problems that probe the limitations of current models in both code generation and formal reasoning. Our evaluation framework replaces ground-truth matching with a formally grounded metric, the equivalence score, and rigorously verifies the quality of generated specifications and code. Our results show that generating formally verifiable code remains a profound challenge for state-of-the-art LLMs. This underscores both the difficulty of the task and the need for benchmarks like VeriEquivBench to drive progress toward scalable and reliable coding agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Bertolazzi","name":"Bertolazzi, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandro","familyName":"Pezzelle","name":"Pezzelle, Sandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raffaella","familyName":"Bernardi","name":"Bernardi, Raffaella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Language Models Conflate Logical Validity with Plausibility: A Representational Analysis of Content Effects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T06:48:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T09:31:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T07:21:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Both humans and large language models (LLMs) exhibit content effects: biases in which the plausibility of the semantic content of a reasoning problem influences judgments regarding its logical validity. While this phenomenon in humans is best explained by the dual-process theory of reasoning, the mechanisms behind content effects in LLMs remain unclear. In this work, we address this issue by investigating how LLMs encode the concepts of validity and plausibility within their internal representations. We show that both concepts are linearly represented and strongly aligned in representational geometry, leading models to conflate plausibility with validity. Using steering vectors, we demonstrate that plausibility vectors can causally bias validity judgements, and vice versa, and that the degree of alignment between these two concepts predicts the magnitude of behavioral content effects across models. Finally, we construct debiasing vectors that disentangle these concepts, reducing content effects and improving reasoning accuracy. Our findings advance understanding of how abstract logical concepts are represented in LLMs and highlight representational interventions as a path toward more logical systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:52:24Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:52:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting-Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ting-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raviteja","familyName":"Vemulapalli","name":"Vemulapalli, Raviteja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hema Swetha","familyName":"Koppula","name":"Koppula, Hema Swetha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Cemri","name":"Cemri, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COMPASS: Benchmarking Constrained Optimization in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:09:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T19:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human decision-making often involves constrained optimization. As LLM agents are deployed to assist with real-world tasks like travel planning, shopping, and scheduling, they must mirror this capability. We introduce COMPASS, a benchmark that evaluates whether LLM agents can perform constrained optimization in realistic travel planning settings. To success in these tasks, agents must engage in multi-turn conversations with user to gather task information as well as use tools to gather information from the database. Then agents must propose a solution that not only satisfies hard constraints but also optimizes user's utility objective. Evaluating state-of-the-art models, we reveal a significant feasible-optimal gap: while models achieve 70-90% feasibility (constraint satisfaction), they reach only 20-60% optimality (utility optimization). Our analysis shows that tool use is not the bottleneck. Instead, the core limitation is insufficient exploration of the search space, with success strongly correlating with information gathered. Coding agents show a promising approach to mitigate this gap. Together, COMPASS provides a testbed for developing LLM agents that can truly mirror human decision-making by both satisfying constraints and optimizing objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minju","familyName":"Gwak","name":"Gwak, Minju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guijin","familyName":"Son","name":"Son, Guijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaehyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting the Uniform Information Density Hypothesis in LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:37:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T03:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T04:43:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T00:25:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Uniform Information Density (UID) hypothesis proposes that effective communication is achieved by maintaining a stable flow of information. In this work, we revisit this principle in the context of Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning, asking whether step-level uniformity reflects reasoning quality. To this end, we introduce a novel framework to quantify uniformity of information flow at both local and global levels, using an entropy-based stepwise density metric. Across experiments on seven reasoning benchmarks, we see a counter-intuitive pattern: while high-quality reasoning exhibit smooth step-by-step transitions local uniformity and structured, non-uniform information flow at the trajectory level global non-uniformity. The results demonstrate that these uniformities outperform alternative internal signals as predictors of reasoning quality, and such divergence with human communication is not a model deficiency, but a byproduct of distinct objectives between human communication and LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-20T02:09:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jusen","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weigao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weigao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Native Hybrid Attention for Efficient Sequence Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T13:24:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers excel at sequence modeling but face quadratic complexity, while linear attention offers improved efficiency but often compromises recall accuracy over long contexts. In this work, we introduce Native Hybrid Attention (NHA), a novel hybrid architecture of linear and full attention that integrates both intra \u0026amp; inter-layer hybridization into a unified layer design. NHA maintains long-term context in key-value slots updated by a linear RNN, and augments them with short-term tokens from a sliding window. A single softmax attention operation is then applied over all keys and values, enabling per-token and per-head context-dependent weighting without requiring additional fusion parameters. The inter-layer behavior is controlled through a single hyperparameter, the sliding window size, which allows smooth adjustment between purely linear and full attention while keeping all layers structurally uniform. Experimental results show that NHA surpasses Transformers and other hybrid baselines on recall-intensive and commonsense reasoning tasks. Furthermore, pretrained LLMs can be structurally hybridized with NHA, achieving competitive accuracy while delivering significant efficiency gains. Code is available at https://github.com/JusenD/NHA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACL 2026, 17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:54Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepeng","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Zhepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jielin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Webscale-RL: Automated Data Pipeline for Scaling RL Data to Pretraining Levels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T22:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:11:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-10T04:08:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-13T00:22:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success through imitation learning on vast text corpora, but this paradigm creates a training-generation gap and limits robust reasoning. Reinforcement learning (RL) offers a more data-efficient solution capable of bridging this gap, yet its application has been constrained by a critical data bottleneck: existing RL datasets are orders of magnitude smaller and less diverse than web-scale pre-training corpora. To address this, we introduce the Webscale-RL pipeline, a scalable data engine that systematically converts large-scale pre-training documents into millions of diverse, verifiable question-answer pairs for RL. Using this pipeline, we construct the Webscale-RL dataset, containing 1.2 million examples across more than 9 domains. Our experiments show that the model trained on this dataset significantly outperforms continual pretraining and strong data refinement baselines across a suite of benchmarks. Notably, RL training with our dataset proves substantially more efficient, achieving the performance of continual pre-training with up to 100$\\times$ fewer tokens. Our work presents a viable path toward scaling RL to pre-training levels, enabling more capable and efficient language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-13T02:08:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Search-R3: Unifying Reasoning and Embedding in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T10:25:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:44:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their remarkable natural language understanding capabilities, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been underutilized for retrieval tasks. We present Search-R3, a novel framework that addresses this limitation by adapting LLMs to generate search embeddings as a direct output of their reasoning process. Our approach exploits LLMs' chain-of-thought capabilities, allowing them to produce more effective embeddings by reasoning step-by-step through complex semantic analyses. We implement this through three complementary mechanisms. (1) a supervised learning stage enables the model's ability to produce quality embeddings, (2) a reinforcement learning (RL) methodology that optimizes embedding generation alongside reasoning, and (3) a specialized RL environment that efficiently handles evolving embedding representations without requiring complete corpus re-encoding at each training iteration. Our extensive evaluations on diverse benchmarks demonstrate that Search-R3 significantly outperforms prior methods by unifying the reasoning and embedding generation processes. This integrated post-training approach represents a substantial advancement in handling complex knowledge-intensive tasks that require both sophisticated reasoning and effective information retrieval. Project page: https://github.com/ytgui/Search-R3"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CHANGELOG: (1) Completed training of Search-R3-Large; (2) Corrected error formulation; (3) Added a `Discussion` section to the appendix. We appreciation to the anonymous reviewers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Lenci","name":"Lenci, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yves","familyName":"Méheust","name":"Méheust, Yves","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Klepikova","name":"Klepikova, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vittorio","familyName":"Di Federico","name":"Di Federico, Vittorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel M.","familyName":"Tartakovsky","name":"Tartakovsky, Daniel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Effects of Wall Roughness on Coupled Flow and Heat Transport in Fractured Media"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Geophysics (physics.geo-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:50:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-14T08:55:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T13:29:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1017/jfm.2026.11403","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heat transfer in fractured media is governed by the interplay between advective transport along rough-walled fractures and conductive transport, both within the fractures and in the surrounding low-permeability matrix. Flow localization induced by aperture heterogeneity, combined with matrix conduction, gives rise to anomalous thermal behavior. To capture these effects, we develop a stochastic modeling framework that couples a time-domain random walk (TDRW) representation of advective and conductive transport in the fractures with a semi-analytical model of conductive heat exchange with the matrix. Matrix trapping times follow a Lévy-Smirnov distribution derived from first-passage theory, capturing the heavy-tailed dynamics typical of fractured systems. Heat flux at the fracture-matrix interface is computed via a nonlocal convolution integral based on Duhamel's principle, accounting for thermal memory effects. The model is validated against analytical benchmarks and finite-element simulations. Monte Carlo simulations over stochastic aperture fields quantify the influence of fracture closure, correlation length, and Péclet number. Results reveal a transition from superdiffusive to subdiffusive regimes, driven by the competition between advective transport along preferential paths, dispersion induced by aperture variability, and matrix-driven heat conduction. In the long-time regime, heat exchange exhibits a characteristic $t^{-1/2}$ decay. At early times, limited thermal penetration into the matrix leads to weaker interfacial fluxes, underscoring the role of matrix thermal inertia. The proposed framework enables physically consistent and computationally efficient simulations of thermal transport in complex fractured systems, with implications for geothermal energy, subsurface thermal storage, and engineered heat exchange in low-permeability environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in Journal of Fluid Mechanics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:19:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06390","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06390","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06390","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Margaret E","familyName":"Carrington","name":"Carrington, Margaret E","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryce T.","familyName":"Friesen","name":"Friesen, Bryce T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doug","familyName":"Pickering","name":"Pickering, Doug","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shane","familyName":"Sangster","name":"Sangster, Shane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaene","familyName":"Soopramania","name":"Soopramania, Kaene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proper time expansions and glasma dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:13:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:05:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-07T14:22:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The earliest phase of an ultrarelativistic heavy ion collision can be described as a highly populated system of gluons called glasma. The system's dynamics is governed by the classical Yang-Mills equation. Solutions can be found at early times using a proper time expansion. Since the expansion parameter is the time, this method is necessarily limited to the study of early time dynamics. In addition compute time and memory limitations restrict practical calculations to no more than eighth order in the expansion. The result is that the method produces reliable results only for very early times. In this paper we explore several different methods to increase the maximum time that can be reached. We find that, depending slightly on the quantity being calculated, the latest time for which reliable results are obtained can be extended approximately 1.5 times (from $\\sim0.05$~fm/$c$ using previous methods to about $0.08$~fm/$c$)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 10 figures, published version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06390","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:11Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:31:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chios","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hehai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heqing","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Heqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FURINA: A Fully Customizable Role-Playing Benchmark via Scalable Multi-Agent Collaboration Pipeline"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T15:48:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:07:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T11:46:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) advance in role-playing (RP) tasks, existing benchmarks quickly become obsolete due to their narrow scope, outdated interaction paradigms, and limited adaptability across diverse application scenarios. To address this gap, we introduce FURINA-Builder, a novel multi-agent collaboration pipeline that automatically constructs fully customizable RP benchmarks at any scale. It enables evaluation of arbitrary characters across diverse scenarios and prompt formats, as the first benchmark builder in RP area for adaptable assessment. FURINA-Builder simulates dialogues between a test character and other characters drawn from a well-constructed character-scene pool, while an LLM judge selects fine-grained evaluation dimensions and adjusts the test character's responses into final test utterances. Using this pipeline, we build FURINA-Bench, a new comprehensive role-playing benchmark featuring both established and synthesized test characters, each assessed with dimension-specific evaluation criteria. Human evaluation and preliminary separability analysis justify our pipeline and benchmark design. We conduct extensive evaluations of cutting-edge LLMs and find that o3 and DeepSeek-R1 achieve the best performance on English and Chinese RP tasks, respectively. Across all models, established characters consistently outperform synthesized ones, with reasoning capabilities further amplifying this disparity. Interestingly, we observe that model scale does not monotonically reduce hallucinations. More critically, for reasoning LLMs, we uncover a novel trade-off: reasoning improves RP performance but simultaneously increases RP hallucinations. This trade-off extends to a broader Pareto frontier between RP performance and reliability for all LLMs. These findings demonstrate the effectiveness of FURINA-Builder and the challenge posed by FURINA-Bench."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:26:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Sercan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bayram","familyName":"Tekin","name":"Tekin, Bayram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Fractional Time Operators Reproduce Gravitational-Wave Memory? A No-Go Result"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:01:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T13:20:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We initiate an investigation into whether fractional calculus, with its intrinsic long-tailed memory and nonlocal features, can provide a viable model for gravitational-wave memory effects. We consider two toy constructions: ($i$) a fractional modification of the linearized Einstein field equations using a sequential Caputo operator; and ($ii$) a fractionalized quadrupole formula in which the same operator acts on the source moment. Both constructions yield history-dependent responses with small memory-like offsets. However, in all cases we studied, the signal decays to zero at late times, failing to reproduce the permanent displacement predicted by General Relativity. We showed that, under asymptotic and spatial flatness of spacetime, the solutions of the proposed models decay to zero at late times when the time derivatives of the perturbed metric are temporally localized and bounded at each spatial point. Therefore, our results constitute a no-go demonstration: naive fractionalization is insufficient to model the permanent offset in the metric without explicitly building in flux-balance laws or asymptotic symmetry structure. We argue that any successful model must incorporate fractional kernels directly into the hereditary flux-balance integral of General Relativity while preserving gauge invariance and dimensional consistency. We also discuss possible connections to modified gravity and the absence of memory in spacetime with $D\u0026gt;4$ dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 6 figures, significantly extended; version to appear in the proceedings of 60 Years in Mathematical Physics: Gurses-Fest issue of Journal of Physics: Conference Series (JPCS, IOP Publishing)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Penghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qianxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Effect of Attention Head Count on Transformer Approximation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:27:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:25:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T07:14:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:33:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer has become the dominant architecture for sequence modeling, yet a detailed understanding of how its structural parameters influence expressive power remains limited. In this work, we study the approximation properties of transformers, with particular emphasis on the role of the number of attention heads. Our analysis begins with the introduction of a generalized $D$-retrieval task, which we prove to be dense in the space of continuous functions, thereby providing the basis for our theoretical framework. We then establish both upper and lower bounds on the parameter complexity required for $ε$-approximation. Specifically, we show that transformers with sufficiently many heads admit efficient approximation, whereas with too few heads, the number of parameters must scale at least as $O(1/ε^{cT})$, for some constant $c$ and sequence length $T$. To the best of our knowledge, this constitutes the first rigorous lower bound of this type in a nonlinear and practically relevant setting. We further examine the single-head case and demonstrate that an embedding dimension of order $O(T)$ allows complete memorization of the input, where approximation is entirely achieved by the feed-forward block. Finally, we validate our theoretical findings with experiments on both synthetic data and real-world tasks, illustrating the practical relevance of our results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06882","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06882","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06882","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Sedlak","name":"Sedlak, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Raith","name":"Raith, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Morichetta","name":"Morichetta, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Víctor Casamayor","familyName":"Pujol","name":"Pujol, Víctor Casamayor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Schahram","familyName":"Dustdar","name":"Dustdar, Schahram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Dimensional Autoscaling of Stream Processing Services on Edge Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T15:35:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:58:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Edge devices have limited resources, which inevitably leads to situations where stream processing services cannot satisfy their needs. While existing autoscaling mechanisms focus entirely on resource scaling, Edge devices require alternative ways to sustain the Service Level Objectives (SLOs) of competing services. To address these issues, we introduce a Multi-dimensional Autoscaling Platform (MUDAP) that supports fine-grained vertical scaling across both service- and resource-level dimensions. MUDAP supports service-specific scaling tailored to available parameters, e.g., scale data quality or model size for a particular service. To optimize the execution across services, we present a scaling agent based on Regression Analysis of Structural Knowledge (RASK). The RASK agent efficiently explores the solution space and learns a continuous regression model of the processing environment for inferring optimal scaling actions. We compared our approach with two autoscalers, the Kubernetes VPA and a reinforcement learning agent, for scaling up to 9 services on a single Edge device. Our results showed that RASK can infer an accurate regression model in merely 20 iterations (i.e., observe 200s of processing). By increasingly adding elasticity dimensions, RASK sustained the highest request load with 28% less SLO violations, compared to baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06882","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":1,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:39Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-30T02:11:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tavish","familyName":"McDonald","name":"McDonald, Tavish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav","familyName":"Fort","name":"Fort, Stanislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhavya","familyName":"Kailkhura","name":"Kailkhura, Bhavya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Bartoldson","name":"Bartoldson, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Get RICH or Die Scaling: Profitably Trading Inference Compute for Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:18:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T18:15:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:48:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T04:07:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:26:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time reasoning has raised benchmark performances and even shown promise in addressing the historically intractable problem of making models robust to adversarially out-of-distribution (OOD) data. Indeed, recent work used reasoning to aid satisfaction of model specifications designed to thwart attacks, finding a striking correlation between LLM reasoning effort and robustness to jailbreaks. However, this benefit fades when stronger (e.g. gradient-based or multimodal) attacks are used. This may be expected as models often can't follow instructions on the adversarially OOD data created by such attacks, and instruction following is needed to act in accordance with the attacker-thwarting spec. Thus, we hypothesize that the test-time robustness benefits of specs are unlocked by initial robustness sufficient to follow instructions on OOD data. Namely, we posit the Robustness from Inference Compute Hypothesis (RICH): inference-compute defenses profit as the model's training data better reflects the components of attacked data. Guided by the RICH, we test models of varying initial-robustness levels, finding inference-compute adds robustness even to white-box multimodal attacks, provided the model has sufficient initial robustness. Further evidencing a rich-get-richer dynamic, InternVL 3.5 gpt-oss 20B gains little robustness when its test compute is scaled, but such scaling adds significant robustness if we first robustify its vision encoder (creating the first adversarially robust reasoning VLM in the process). Robustifying models makes attacked components of data more in-distribution (ID), and the RICH suggests this fuels compositional generalization -- understanding OOD data via its ID components -- to following spec instructions on adversarial data. Consistently, we find test-time defenses both build and depend on train-time data and defenses."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:30Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicong","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Sicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Lobefaro","name":"Lobefaro, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moein","familyName":"Taherkhani","name":"Taherkhani, Moein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuying","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohit","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Rohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cyrill","familyName":"Stachniss","name":"Stachniss, Cyrill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maren","familyName":"Bennewitz","name":"Bennewitz, Maren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient View Planning Guided by Previous-Session Reconstruction for Repeated Plant Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:57:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T02:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:25:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Repeated plant monitoring is essential for tracking crop growth, and 3D reconstruction enables consistent comparison across monitoring sessions. However, rebuilding a 3D model from scratch in every session is costly and overlooks informative geometry already observed previously. We propose efficient view planning guided by a previous-session reconstruction, which reuses a 3D model from the previous session to improve active perception in the current session. Based on this previous-session reconstruction, our method replaces iterative next-best-view planning with one-shot view planning that selects an informative set of views and computes the globally shortest execution path connecting them. Experiments on real multi-session datasets, including public single-plant scans and a newly collected greenhouse crop-row dataset, show that our method achieves comparable or higher surface coverage with fewer executed views and shorter robot paths than iterative and one-shot baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted for review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:08Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06638","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06638","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wenkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingtong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weicheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaR-KVQA: Structured Reasoning Traces for Implicit-Knowledge Visual Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:37:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:23:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T09:26:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:29:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-22T14:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge-based Visual Question Answering (KVQA) requires models to ground entities in images and reason over factual knowledge. Recent work has introduced its implicit-knowledge variant, IK-KVQA, where a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is the sole knowledge source and answers are produced without external retrieval. Existing IK-KVQA approaches, however, are typically trained with answer-only supervision: reasoning remains implicit, justifications are often weak or inconsistent, and generalization after standard supervised fine-tuning (SFT) can be brittle. We propose StaR-KVQA, a framework that equips IK-KVQA with dual-path structured reasoning traces - symbolic relation paths over text and vision together with path-grounded natural-language explanations - to provide a stronger inductive bias than generic answer-only supervision. These traces act as modality-aware scaffolds that guide the model toward relevant entities and attributes, offering more structure than generic chain-of-thought supervision while not constraining reasoning to any single fixed path. With a single open-source MLLM, StaR-KVQA constructs and selects traces to build an offline trace-enriched dataset and then performs structure-aware self-distillation; no external retrievers, verifiers, or curated knowledge bases are used, and inference is a single autoregressive pass. Across benchmarks, StaR-KVQA consistently improves both answer accuracy and the transparency of intermediate reasoning, achieving up to +11.3% higher answer accuracy on OK-VQA over the strongest baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8+3+3 pages, code: https://github.com/jianyingzhihe/StaR-KVQA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06638","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyun","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Changyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resilient Multi-Dimensional Consensus and Distributed Optimization against Agent-Based and Denial-of-Service Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T10:32:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:46:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:05:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:12:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we consider the resilient multi-dimensional consensus and distributed optimization problems of multi-agent systems (MASs) in the presence of both agent-based and denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. The considered agent-based attacks can cover malicious, Byzantine, and stubborn agents. The links between agents in the network can be blocked by DoS attacks, which may lead the digraph to be time-varying and even disconnected. The objective is to ensure that the remaining benign agents achieve consensus. To this end, an \"auxiliary point\"-based resilient control algorithm is proposed for MASs. Under the proposed algorithm, each healthy agent constructs a \"safe kernel\" utilizing the states of its in-neighbors and updates its state toward a specific point within this kernel at each iteration. If an agent cannot receive its neighbors' states owing to DoS attacks, it will use the states received immediately before the DoS period. Moreover, a resilient multi-dimensional distributed optimization (RMDO) algorithm is also proposed. Theoretical proofs and numerical examples are presented to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06436","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06436","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06436","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas Marshall","familyName":"Vielmetti","name":"Vielmetti, Thomas Marshall","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devansh R.","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Devansh R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitra","familyName":"Panagou","name":"Panagou, Dimitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"R3R: Decentralized Multi-Agent Collision Avoidance with Infinite-Horizon Safety"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:13:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:07:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-15T16:11:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing decentralized methods for multi-agent motion planning lack formal, infinite-horizon safety guarantees, especially for communication-constrained systems. We present R3R which, to our knowledge, is the first decentralized and asynchronous framework for multi-agent motion planning under range-limited communication constraints with infinite-horizon safety guarantees for systems of nonlinear agents. R3R's novelty lies in combining our gatekeeper safety framework with a geometric constraint termed R-Boundedness, which together establish a formal link between an agent's communication radius and its ability to plan safely. We constrain trajectories to lie within a fixed planning radius, determined by a function of the agent's communication radius. This enables trajectories to be certified as provably safe for all time using only local information. Our algorithm is fully asynchronous, and ensures the forward invariance of these guarantees even in time-varying networks where agents asynchronously join and replan. We evaluate our approach in simulations of up to 128 Dubins vehicles, validating our theoretical safety guarantees in dense, obstacle-rich scenarios. We further show that R3R's computational complexity scales with local agent density rather than problem size, providing a practical solution for scalable and provably safe multi-agent systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, LaTeX; submitted to the American Control Conference (ACC) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06436","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:15Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masato","familyName":"Nozawa","name":"Nozawa, Masato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Torii","name":"Torii, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kerr-Schild transformation of the Benenti-Francaviglia metric"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:25:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T07:04:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:50:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/62b3-kqcw","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Benenti-Francaviglia (BF) family of metrics provides the most general form of a spacetime metric that admits two mutually commuting Killing vectors and an irreducible Killing tensor. The geodesic equations for the BF family are thus completely integrable by separation of variables. Within this broad class, we explore the Kerr-Schild transformation of a degenerate subclass distinguished by the existence of a shear-free null geodesic congruence. By requiring the deformed metric to preserve the Killing symmetry and circularity, we demonstrate that the deformed metric again falls into the degenerate BF family, modulo the replacement of a single structure function. We apply the present algorithm to ${\\cal N}=2$ gauged supergravity and obtain a dyonic generalization of the Chong-Cvetič-Lü-Pope rotating black hole solution, by taking the background metric to be a solution of the Einstein-scalar gravity. The present prescription extends to five dimensions, provided that the constant of geodesic motion associated with the extra Killing direction vanishes. The same reasoning applies to the case where the background degenerate BF metric is distorted in a (non)conformal manner. Our formalism offers a unified perspective on the relation between seed and deformed metrics in the Kerr-Schild construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages; v2: minor modifications, to appear in PRD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:08Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanke","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhanke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Chentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zongze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangchao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Jiangchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghangfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianghangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tangyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Tangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linrui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Linrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brando","familyName":"Miranda","name":"Miranda, Brando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanmi","familyName":"Koyejo","name":"Koyejo, Sanmi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AlphaApollo: A System for Deep Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:42:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:25:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present AlphaApollo, an agentic reasoning system that targets two bottlenecks in foundation-model reasoning: (1) limited reasoning capacity for complex, long-horizon problem solving and (2) unreliable test-time evolution without trustworthy verification. AlphaApollo orchestrates models and tools via three components: (i) multi-turn agentic reasoning, which formalizes model-environment interaction with structured tool calls and responses; (ii) multi-turn agentic learning, which applies turn-level reinforcement learning to optimize tool-use reasoning while decoupling actions from tool responses for stable training; and (iii) multi-round agentic evolution, which refines solutions through a propose-judge-update loop with tool-assisted verifications and long-horizon memory. Across seven math reasoning benchmarks and multiple model scales, AlphaApollo improves performance through reliable tool use (\u0026gt; 85% tool-call success), substantial gains from multi-turn RL (Avg@32: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct 1.07% -\u0026gt; 9.64%, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct 8.77% -\u0026gt; 20.35%), and improvements from evolution (e.g., Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct 5.27% -\u0026gt; 7.70%, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct 16.53% -\u0026gt; 21.08%). This project is still ongoing. We welcome feedback from the community and will frequently update the source code and technical report."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Ongoing project"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enshen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Enshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanyu","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Shanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanghang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TIGeR: Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning in Vision-Language Models for Robotics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:58:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:56:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T07:07:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities in spatial reasoning, yet they remain fundamentally limited to qualitative precision and lack the computational precision required for real-world robotics. Current approaches fail to leverage metric cues from depth sensors and camera calibration, instead reducing geometric problems to pattern recognition tasks that cannot deliver the centimeter-level accuracy essential for robotic manipulation. We present TIGeR (Tool-Integrated Geometric Reasoning), a novel framework that transforms VLMs from perceptual estimators to geometric computers by enabling them to generate and execute precise geometric computations through external tools. Rather than attempting to internalize complex geometric operations within neural networks, TIGeR empowers models to recognize geometric reasoning requirements, synthesize appropriate computational code, and invoke specialized libraries for exact calculations. To support this paradigm, we introduce TIGeR-300K, a comprehensive tool-invocation-oriented dataset covering point transformations, pose estimation, and spatial compatibility verification, complete with tool invocation sequences and intermediate computations. Through a two-stage training pipeline combining supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement fine-tuning (RFT) with our proposed hierarchical reward design, TIGeR achieves SOTA performance on geometric reasoning benchmarks while demonstrating centimeter-level precision in real-world robotic manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor","familyName":"Cherepanov","name":"Cherepanov, Egor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey K.","familyName":"Kovalev","name":"Kovalev, Alexey K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleksandr I.","familyName":"Panov","name":"Panov, Aleksandr I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ELMUR: External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite for Long-Horizon RL Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:50:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T17:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:14:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Real-world robotic agents must act under partial observability and long horizons, where key cues may appear long before they affect decision making. However, most modern approaches rely solely on instantaneous information, without incorporating insights from the past. Standard recurrent or transformer models struggle with retaining and leveraging long-term dependencies: context windows truncate history, while naive memory extensions fail under scale and sparsity. We propose ELMUR (External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite), a transformer architecture with structured external memory. Each layer maintains memory embeddings, interacts with them via bidirectional cross-attention, and updates them through an Least Recently Used (LRU) memory module using replacement or convex blending. ELMUR extends effective horizons up to 100,000 times beyond the attention window and achieves a 100% success rate on a synthetic T-Maze task with corridors up to one million steps. In POPGym, it outperforms baselines on more than half of the tasks. On MIKASA-Robo sparse-reward manipulation tasks with visual observations, it nearly doubles the performance of strong baselines, achieving the best success rate on 21 out of 23 tasks and improving the aggregate success rate across all tasks by about 70% over the previous best baseline. These results demonstrate that structured, layer-local external memory offers a simple and scalable approach to decision making under partial observability. Code and project page: https://elmur-paper.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 15 figures, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aochong Oliver","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Aochong Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanya","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Tanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Off-Trajectory Reasoning: Can LLMs Collaborate on Reasoning Trajectory?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:42:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning LLMs are trained to verbalize their reasoning process, yielding strong gains on complex tasks. This transparency also opens a promising direction: multiple reasoners can directly collaborate on each other's thinking within a shared trajectory, yielding better inference efficiency and exploration. A key prerequisite, however, is the ability to assess the usefulness and build on another model's partial thinking -- we call this off-trajectory reasoning. Our paper investigates a critical question: can standard solo-reasoning training pipelines deliver desired off-trajectory behaviors? We propose twin tests that capture the two extremes of the off-trajectory spectrum, namely Recoverability, which tests whether LLMs can backtrack from \"distractions\" induced by misleading reasoning traces, and Guidability, which tests their ability to build upon correct reasoning from stronger collaborators. Our study evaluates 15 open-weight LLMs (1.5B-32B) and reveals a counterintuitive finding -- \"stronger\" LLMs on benchmarks are often more fragile under distraction. Moreover, all models tested fail to effectively leverage guiding steps from collaborators on problems beyond their inherent capabilities with solve rates remaining under 9.2%. Finally, we conduct control studies to isolate the effects of three factors in post-training on these behaviors: the choice of distillation teacher, the use of RL, and data selection strategy. Our results provide actionable insights for training natively strong reasoning collaborators; e.g., we find that suboptimal recoverability behaviors of teacher models are transferred to distilled students even if the distillation trajectories are correct. Taken together, this work lays the groundwork for evaluating multi-model collaborations in shared reasoning trajectories and highlights the limitations of off-the-shelf reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07233","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07233","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhivar","familyName":"Sourati","name":"Sourati, Zhivar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne Menglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Marianne Menglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yazhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Mengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeeth","familyName":"Bharadwaj","name":"Bharadwaj, Sujeeth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morteza","familyName":"Dehghani","name":"Dehghani, Morteza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Roth","name":"Roth, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAD-RAG: Layout-aware Dynamic RAG for Visually-Rich Document Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:02:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T00:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question answering over visually rich documents (VRDs) requires reasoning not only over isolated content but also over documents' structural organization and cross-page dependencies. However, conventional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods encode content in isolated chunks during ingestion, losing structural and cross-page dependencies, and retrieve a fixed number of pages at inference, regardless of the specific demands of the question or context. This often results in incomplete evidence retrieval and degraded answer quality for multi-page reasoning tasks. To address these limitations, we propose LAD-RAG, a novel Layout-Aware Dynamic RAG framework. During ingestion, LAD-RAG constructs a symbolic document graph that captures layout structure and cross-page dependencies, adding it alongside standard neural embeddings to yield a more holistic representation of the document. During inference, an LLM agent dynamically interacts with the neural and symbolic indices to adaptively retrieve the necessary evidence based on the query. Experiments on MMLongBench-Doc, LongDocURL, DUDE, and MP-DocVQA demonstrate that LAD-RAG improves retrieval, achieving over 90% perfect recall on average without any top-k tuning, and outperforming baseline retrievers by up to 20% in recall at comparable noise levels, yielding higher QA accuracy with minimal latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07233","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06823","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06823","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riku","familyName":"Mochizuki","name":"Mochizuki, Riku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusuke","familyName":"Komatsu","name":"Komatsu, Shusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souta","familyName":"Noguchi","name":"Noguchi, Souta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuto","familyName":"Ataka","name":"Ataka, Kazuto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exposing Citation Vulnerabilities in Generative Engines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:47:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T03:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyze answers generated by generative engines (GEs) from the perspectives of citation publishers and the content-injection barrier, defined as the difficulty for attackers to manipulate answers to user prompts by placing malicious content on the web. GEs integrate two functions: web search and answer generation that cites web pages using large language models. Because anyone can publish information on the web, GEs are vulnerable to poisoning attacks. Existing studies of citation evaluation focus on how faithfully answer content reflects cited sources, leaving unexamined which web sources should be selected as citations to defend against poisoning attacks. To fill this gap, we introduce evaluation criteria that assess poisoning threats using the citation information contained in answers. Our criteria classify the publisher attributes of citations to estimate the content-injection barrier thereby revealing the threat of poisoning attacks in current GEs. We conduct experiments in political domains in Japan and the United States (U.S.) using our criteria and show that citations from official party websites (primary sources) are approximately \\(25\\%\\)--\\(45\\%\\) in the U.S. and \\(60\\%\\)--\\(65\\%\\) in Japan, indicating that U.S. political answers are at higher risk of poisoning attacks. We also find that sources with low content-injection barriers are frequently cited yet are poorly reflected in answer content. To mitigate this threat, we discuss how publishers of primary sources can increase exposure of their web content in answers and show that well-known techniques are limited by language differences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, under-reviewing at a conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06823","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:16Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Ranjan","name":"Ranjan, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valter","familyName":"Hudovernik","name":"Hudovernik, Valter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Znidar","name":"Znidar, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charilaos","familyName":"Kanatsoulis","name":"Kanatsoulis, Charilaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshan","familyName":"Upendra","name":"Upendra, Roshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmoud","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mahmoud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joe","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, Joe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom","familyName":"Palczewski","name":"Palczewski, Tom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos","familyName":"Guestrin","name":"Guestrin, Carlos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jure","familyName":"Leskovec","name":"Leskovec, Jure","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relational Transformer: Toward Zero-Shot Foundation Models for Relational Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:51:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T22:13:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:10:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T07:22:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:45:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained transformers readily adapt to new sequence modeling tasks via zero-shot prompting, but relational domains still lack architectures that transfer across datasets and tasks. The core challenge is the diversity of relational data, with varying heterogeneous schemas, graph structures and functional dependencies. In this paper, we present the Relational Transformer (RT) architecture, which can be pretrained on diverse relational databases and directly applied to unseen datasets and tasks without task- or dataset-specific fine-tuning, or retrieval of in-context examples. RT (i) incorporates task specification via task table prompting, (ii) tokenizes cells with table/column metadata, (iii) is pretrained via masked token prediction, and (iv) utilizes a novel Relational Attention mechanism over columns, rows, and primary-foreign key links. Pretrained on RelBench datasets spanning tasks such as churn and sales forecasting, RT attains strong zero-shot performance, averaging 93% of fully supervised AUROC on binary classification tasks with a single forward pass of a 22M parameter model, as opposed to 84% for a 27B LLM. Fine-tuning yields state-of-the-art results with high sample efficiency. Our experimental analyses show that RT's zero-shot transfer leverages task context, relational attention patterns and schema semantics. Overall, RT provides a practical path toward foundation models for relational data. Code, models, data: https://github.com/snap-stanford/relational-transformer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06292","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06292","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yike","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChainMPQ: Interleaved Text-Image Reasoning Chains for Mitigating Relation Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:19:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T01:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:56:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal tasks, hallucinations continue to hinder their reliability. Among the three categories of hallucinations, which include object, attribute, and relation, relation hallucinations account for the largest proportion but have received the least attention. To address this issue, we propose ChainMPQ (Multi-Perspective Questions guided Interleaved Chain of Image and Text), a training-free method that improves relational inference in LVLMs by utilizing accumulated textual and visual memories. ChainMPQ first extracts subject and object keywords from the question to enhance the corresponding image regions. It then constructs multi-perspective questions that focus on the three core components of a relationship: the subject, the object, and the relation that links them. These questions are sequentially input to the model, with textual and visual memories from earlier steps providing supporting context for subsequent ones, thereby forming an interleaved chain of images and text that guides progressive relational reasoning. Experiments on multiple LVLMs and benchmarks show that ChainMPQ substantially reduces relation hallucinations, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of its three core modules."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06292","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kelvin Kiu-Wai","familyName":"Tam","name":"Tam, Kelvin Kiu-Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newt Hue-Nam K.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Newt Hue-Nam K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqing","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Tianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ginny Y.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ginny Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"See","name":"See, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NewtonBench: Benchmarking Generalizable Scientific Law Discovery in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:12:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T09:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:11:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are emerging as powerful tools for scientific law discovery, a foundational challenge in AI-driven science. However, existing benchmarks for this task suffer from a fundamental methodological trilemma, forcing a trade-off between scientific relevance, scalability, and resistance to memorization. Furthermore, they oversimplify discovery as static function fitting, failing to capture the authentic scientific process of uncovering embedded laws through the interactive exploration of complex model systems. To address these critical gaps, we introduce NewtonBench, a benchmark comprising 324 scientific law discovery tasks across 12 physics domains. Our design mitigates the evaluation trilemma by using counterfactual law shifts - systematic alterations of canonical laws - to generate a vast suite of problems that are scalable, scientifically relevant, and memorization-resistant. Moreover, we elevate the evaluation from static function fitting to interactive model discovery, requiring agents to experimentally probe simulated complex systems to uncover hidden principles. Our extensive experiment reveals a clear but fragile capability for discovery in frontier LLMs: this ability degrades precipitously with increasing system complexity and exhibits extreme sensitivity to observational noise. Notably, we uncover a paradoxical effect of tool assistance: providing a code interpreter can hinder more capable models by inducing a premature shift from exploration to exploitation, causing them to satisfice on suboptimal solutions. These results demonstrate that robust, generalizable discovery in complex, interactive environments remains the core challenge. By providing a scalable, robust, and scientifically authentic testbed, NewtonBench offers a crucial tool for measuring true progress and guiding the development of next-generation AI agents capable of genuine scientific discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mitchell Keren","familyName":"Taraday","name":"Taraday, Mitchell Keren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahaf","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Shahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaim","familyName":"Baskin","name":"Baskin, Chaim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Discriminative Joint Encoders for Large Scale Vision-Language Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:46:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-22T12:55:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal retrieval still leans on embedding-based models like CLIP for fast vector search over pre-computed image embeddings. Yet, unlike text retrieval, where joint-encoder rerankers are standard, comparable vision-language rerankers are largely absent. We find that seminal joint encoders such as BLIP are severely bottlenecked by an expensive visual feature-extraction stage, preventing practical deployment at scale. Motivated by this bottleneck, we introduce EDJE, an Efficient Discriminative Joint Encoder that precomputes vision tokens offline and compresses them via a lightweight attention-based adapter, so online inference runs only a compact joint encoder over a small set of visual tokens plus the text. EDJE preserves strong retrieval performance while drastically reducing storage and online compute, enabling high-throughput inference. Specifically, EDJE processes 50k image--text pairs/second while requiring 49kB of disk storage per image, matching prior art on Flickr (zero-shot) and COCO (fine-tuned) retrieval."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:12Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marzia","familyName":"Bisi","name":"Bisi, Marzia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martina","familyName":"Conte","name":"Conte, Martina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Groppi","name":"Groppi, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kinetic modeling of knowledge and wealth dynamics in national and global markets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35Q84, 82C40, 91B80","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:08:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:09:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-21T23:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1142/s0218202526410034","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a kinetic model to describe the dynamical evolution of wealth and knowledge in national and global markets, starting from a microscopic description of individual interactions. The model is built upon interaction rules that account for a strong interdependence between the microscopic variables, influencing agents' trading and saving propensities, knowledge acquisition, and the stochastic market effects. We begin with a domestic market scenario and extend the framework to international trade, incorporating the possibility of individual transfers between different countries. The dynamics of the system are described through Boltzmann-type equations, which allow for a detailed study of the evolution of the agent distribution in each country. In this context, we study the evolution of macroscopic quantities of the system, focusing on the number density of individuals, and the mean wealth and knowledge of each population, and we discuss these results in relation to existing models in the literature. Finally, under a quasi-invariant trading limit, we derive simplified Fokker-Planck type equations that reveal some emergent behaviors of the system, including the formation of Pareto tails in the long-term wealth and knowledge distributions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:58Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07117","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07117","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07117","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Christov-Moore","name":"Christov-Moore, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Juliani","name":"Juliani, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Kiefer","name":"Kiefer, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Lehman","name":"Lehman, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicco","familyName":"Reggente","name":"Reggente, Nicco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. Scot","familyName":"Rousse","name":"Rousse, B. Scot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Safron","name":"Safron, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolás","familyName":"Hinrichs","name":"Hinrichs, Nicolás","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Polani","name":"Polani, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Damasio","name":"Damasio, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Conditions of Physical Embodiment Enable Generalization and Care"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:10:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:53:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T11:06:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:33:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T19:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As artificial agents enter open-ended physical environments -- eldercare, disaster response, and space missions -- they must persist under uncertainty while providing reliable care. Yet current systems struggle to generalize across distribution shifts and lack intrinsic motivation to preserve the well-being of others. Vulnerability and mortality are often seen as constraints to be avoided, yet organisms survive and provide care in an open-ended world with relative ease and efficiency. We argue that generalization and care arise from conditions of physical embodiment: being-in-the-world (the agent is a part of the environment) and being-towards-death (unless counteracted, the agent drifts toward terminal states). These conditions necessitate a homeostatic drive to maintain oneself and maximize the future capacity to continue doing so. Fulfilling this drive over long time horizons in multi-agent environments necessitates robust causal modeling of self and others' embodiment and jointly achievable future states. Because embodied agents are part of the environment, with the self delimited by reliable control, empowering others can expand self-boundaries, enabling other-regard. This provides a path from embodiment toward generalization and care based in shared constraints. We outline a reinforcement-learning framework for examining these questions. Homeostatic mortal agents continually learning in open-ended environments may offer efficient robustness and trustworthy alignment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07117","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:17Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T03:00:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannick","familyName":"Strocka","name":"Strocka, Yannick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Belhassen","name":"Belhassen, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Schröder","name":"Schröder, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregor","familyName":"Pieplow","name":"Pieplow, Gregor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Software Framework for Optically Accessible Quantum Memory Using Group-IV Color Centers in Diamond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:13:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T07:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T15:38:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:04:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1002/qute.202500681","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the rapidly evolving field of quantum technology, the precise and detailed description of quantum components is not just a necessity but the foundation for advancing research, development, and applications. Optically accessible quantum memories are key building blocks for devices such as quantum repeaters and two-factor authentication. The memory we describe here is based on a tin-vacancy color center coupled to a highly efficient cavity. It leverages state-dependent reflection from the cavity and implements high-fidelity fractional single qubit gates via a train of optical $π/8$ pulses. We also describe its operation under microwave control, further extending our analysis. Our primary contribution in this work is the integration of this device model into a standardized software framework for quantum memory architectures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T03:00:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gulia","name":"Gulia, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amlan","familyName":"Ganguly","name":"Ganguly, Amlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael E.","familyName":"Kuhl","name":"Kuhl, Michael E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Rashedi","name":"Rashedi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clark","familyName":"Hochgraf","name":"Hochgraf, Clark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Augmented Generative AI for Real-time Wireless Prediction in Dynamic Industrial Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-22T15:07:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:54:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T17:26:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T03:07:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate and real-time prediction of wireless channel conditions, particularly the Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR), is a foundational requirement for enabling Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) in highly dynamic Industry 4.0 environments. Traditional physics-based or statistical models fail to cope with the spatio-temporal complexities introduced by mobile obstacles and transient interference inherent to smart warehouses. To address this, we introduce Evo-WISVA (Evolutionary Wireless Infrastructure for Smart Warehouse using VAE), a novel synergistic deep learning architecture that functions as a lightweight 2D predictive digital twin of the radio environment. Evo-WISVA integrates a memory-augmented Variational Autoencoder (VAE) featuring an Attention-driven Latent Memory Module (LMM) for robust, context-aware spatial feature extraction, with a Convolutional Long Short-Term Memory (ConvLSTM) network for precise temporal forecasting and sequential refinement. The entire pipeline is optimized end-to-end via a joint loss function, ensuring optimal feature alignment between the generative and predictive components. Rigorous experimental evaluation conducted on a high-fidelity ns-3-generated industrial warehouse dataset demonstrates that Evo-WISVA significantly surpasses state-of-the-art baselines, achieving up to a 47.6\\% reduction in average reconstruction error. Crucially, the model exhibits exceptional generalization capacity to unseen environments with vastly increased dynamic complexity (up to ten simultaneously moving obstacles) while maintaining amortized computational efficiency essential for real-time deployment. Evo-WISVA establishes a foundational technology for proactive wireless resource management, enabling autonomous optimization and advancing the realization of predictive digital twins in industrial communication networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Found a fundamental error (data leakage) in cross-validation setup affecting both papers. This issue compromises the model training and results, making performance claims unreliable and potentially misleading. We request withdrawal of current versions (v1) to prevent the dissemination of incorrect scientific findings. Corrected versions will be submitted later"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:42Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Pfaffmann","name":"Pfaffmann, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Klusch","name":"Klusch, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Steinmetz","name":"Steinmetz, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HyPlan: Hybrid Learning-Assisted Planning Under Uncertainty for Safe Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:44:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-06T13:14:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:42:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel hybrid learning-assisted planning method, named HyPlan, for solving the collision-free navigation problem for self-driving cars in partially observable traffic environments. HyPlan combines methods for multi-agent behavior prediction, deep reinforcement learning with proximal policy optimization and approximated online POMDP planning with heuristic confidence-based vertical pruning to reduce its execution time without compromising safety of driving. Our experimental performance analysis on the CARLA-CTS2 benchmark of critical traffic scenarios with pedestrians revealed that HyPlan may navigate safer than selected relevant baselines and perform significantly faster than considered alternative online POMDP planners."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06924","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06924","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Almas","familyName":"Baimagambetov","name":"Baimagambetov, Almas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Avgerinakis","name":"Avgerinakis, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Polatidis","name":"Polatidis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ethical AI prompt recommendations in large language models using collaborative filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:03:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) shape AI development, ensuring ethical prompt recommendations is crucial. LLMs offer innovation but risk bias, fairness issues, and accountability concerns. Traditional oversight methods struggle with scalability, necessitating dynamic solutions. This paper proposes using collaborative filtering, a technique from recommendation systems, to enhance ethical prompt selection. By leveraging user interactions, it promotes ethical guidelines while reducing bias. Contributions include a synthetic dataset for prompt recommendations and the application of collaborative filtering. The work also tackles challenges in ethical AI, such as bias mitigation, transparency, and preventing unethical prompt engineering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to by the International Journal of Parallel, Emergent \u0026amp; Distributed Systems (Taylor and Francis) and has an assigned DOI. We have already chose to make this open access using CC BY. The article is not yet available online on the publisher's website. The DOI is: doi.org/10.1080/17445760.2025.2573086"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06924","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:36:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DecompGAIL: Learning Realistic Traffic Behaviors with Decomposed Multi-Agent Generative Adversarial Imitation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T14:53:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:33:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Realistic traffic simulation is critical for the development of autonomous driving systems and urban mobility planning, yet existing imitation learning approaches often fail to model realistic traffic behaviors. Behavior cloning suffers from covariate shift, while Generative Adversarial Imitation Learning (GAIL) is notoriously unstable in multi-agent settings. We identify a key source of this instability: irrelevant interaction misguidance, where a discriminator penalizes an ego vehicle's realistic behavior due to unrealistic interactions among its neighbors. To address this, we propose Decomposed Multi-agent GAIL (DecompGAIL), which explicitly decomposes realism into ego-map and ego-neighbor components, filtering out misleading neighbor: neighbor and neighbor: map interactions. We further introduce a social PPO objective that augments ego rewards with distance-weighted neighborhood rewards, encouraging overall realism across agents. Integrated into a lightweight SMART-based backbone, DecompGAIL achieves state-of-the-art performance on the WOMD Sim Agents 2025 benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted by ICLR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:36:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07238","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07238","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunyi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xunyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Dingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Benchmarks Age: Temporal Misalignment through Large Language Model Factuality Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:06:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T17:39:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) and the real world has outpaced the static nature of widely used evaluation benchmarks, raising concerns about their reliability for evaluating LLM factuality. While substantial works continue to rely on the popular but old benchmarks, their temporal misalignment with real-world facts and modern LLMs, and their effects on LLM factuality evaluation remain underexplored. Therefore, in this work, we present a systematic investigation of this issue by examining five popular factuality benchmarks and eight LLMs released across different years. An up-to-date fact retrieval pipeline and three metrics are tailored to quantify benchmark aging and its impact on LLM factuality evaluation. Experimental results and analysis illustrate that a considerable portion of samples in the widely used factuality benchmarks are outdated, leading to unreliable assessments of LLM factuality. We hope our work can provide a testbed to assess the reliability of a benchmark for LLM factuality evaluation and inspire more research on the benchmark aging issue. Codes are available in https://github.com/JiangXunyi/BenchAge."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EACL 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07238","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Qihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Handong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Handong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushal","familyName":"Kafle","name":"Kafle, Kushal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Kuen","name":"Kuen, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihong","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Zhihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoT Referring: Improving Referring Expression Tasks with Grounded Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:50:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:23:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Referring Expression Comprehension and Segmentation are critical tasks for assessing the integration of language understanding and image comprehension, serving as benchmarks for Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) capabilities. To address these challenges, we propose a new strategy, CoT Referring, which enhances model reasoning across modalities through a structured, chain-of-thought training data structure. Our approach systematically parses textual structures to a sequential referring step, where in each step it identifies relationships and ensures consistent reference alignment, thereby improving accuracy in complex query scenarios. We restructure the training data to enforce a new output form, providing new annotations for existing datasets and compiling an evaluation benchmark from existing resources. This benchmark is designed explicitly for complex referring cases. We also integrate detection and segmentation capabilities into a unified MLLM framework, training it with a novel adaptive weighted loss to optimize performance. Experimental results on our curated benchmark and RefCOCO/+/g demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with a notable increase of 2.5%+ over baseline models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MLLM, Referring Expression Segmentation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahe","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiahe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijay","familyName":"Paladugu","name":"Paladugu, Abhijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beneficial Reasoning Behaviors in Agentic Search and Effective Post-training to Obtain Them"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T06:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T18:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:49:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic search requires large language models (LLMs) to perform multi-step search to solve complex information-seeking tasks, imposing unique challenges on their reasoning capabilities. However, what constitutes effective reasoning for agentic search and how it can be learned remains unclear. In this work, we first investigate the reasoning behaviors that enable success in agentic search. By comparing successful and failed trajectories via an LLM-based analysis pipeline, we identify four beneficial behaviors: Information Verification, Authority Evaluation, Adaptive Search, and Error Recovery. Building on this, we propose Behavior Priming, a training approach that equips agentic search models with these reasoning behaviors before reinforcement learning (RL). Specifically, it first performs supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on collected trajectories exhibiting the identified behaviors to cultivate these behaviors, and then applies standard RL to further improve task performance. Experiments on Qwen3-1.7B and Llama3.2-3B-Instruct show that Behavior Priming yields relative improvements over direct RL by 37.2\\% on three web benchmarks and 6.2\\% on seven multi-hop QA benchmarks, and outperforms the SFT-then-RL baseline using outcome-correct trajectories for fine-tuning. Crucially, we show that these reasoning behaviors matter more than outcome correctness in the priming stage prior to RL. Further analysis reveals that Behavior Priming enhances exploration (pass@8) and test-time scaling (search step number), providing a robust foundation for RL. Our code are avalible at https://github.com/cxcscmu/Behavior-Priming-for-Agentic-Search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07309","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07309","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Hommel","name":"Hommel, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuf Denizay","familyName":"Dönder","name":"Dönder, Yusuf Denizay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungyong","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Sungyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Mimno","name":"Mimno, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Unso Eun Seo","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Unso Eun Seo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent Bain vs. Agent McKinsey: A New Text-to-SQL Benchmark for the Business Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:57:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-11T00:42:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:37:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-13T22:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-SQL benchmarks have traditionally only tested simple data access as a translation task of natural language to SQL queries. But in reality, users tend to ask diverse questions that require more complex responses including data-driven predictions or recommendations. Using the business domain as a motivating example, we introduce CORGI, a new benchmark that expands text-to-SQL to reflect practical database queries encountered by end users. CORGI is composed of synthetic databases inspired by enterprises such as DoorDash, Airbnb, and Lululemon. It provides questions across four increasingly complicated categories of business queries: descriptive, explanatory, predictive, and recommendational. This challenge calls for causal reasoning, temporal forecasting, and strategic recommendation, reflecting multi-level and multi-step agentic intelligence. We find that LLM performance degrades on higher-level questions as question complexity increases. CORGI also introduces and encourages the text-to-SQL community to consider new automatic methods for evaluating open-ended, qualitative responses in data access tasks. Our experiments show that LLMs exhibit an average 33.12% lower success execution rate (SER) on CORGI compared to existing benchmarks such as BIRD, highlighting the substantially higher complexity of real-world business needs. We release the CORGI dataset, an evaluation framework, and a submission website to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, under review for ACL ARR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:51Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inductive Learning for Possibilistic Logic Programs Under Stable Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:32:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:45:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1017/s1471068425100355","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Possibilistic logic programs (poss-programs) under stable models are a major variant of answer set programming (ASP). While its semantics (possibilistic stable models) and properties have been well investigated, the problem of inductive reasoning has not been investigated yet. This paper presents an approach to extracting poss-programs from a background program and examples (parts of intended possibilistic stable models). To this end, the notion of induction tasks is first formally defined, its properties are investigated and two algorithms ilpsm and ilpsmmin for computing induction solutions are presented. An implementation of ilpsmmin is also provided and experimental results show that when inputs are ordinary logic programs, the prototype outperforms a major inductive learning system for normal logic programs from stable models on the datasets that are randomly generated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under consideration in Theory and Practice of Logic Programming (TPLP)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:08Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Linyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization: Synergizing Reasoning and Adaptive Tool Use with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T08:14:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:09:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have popularized test-time scaling, where models generate additional reasoning tokens before producing final answers. These approaches have demonstrated significant performance improvements on benchmarks involving mathematical reasoning. However, language models relying solely on direct inference still struggle with tasks demanding up-to-date knowledge or computational tools such as calculators and code interpreters for complex arithmetic operations. To overcome these limitations, we propose Tool-Augmented Policy Optimization (TAPO), a novel reinforcement learning framework that systematically integrates multi-hop reasoning with adaptive tool-calling capabilities. Our approach employs a modified version of Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization (DAPO), a recently developed RL paradigm, which we adapt specifically for tool invocation scenarios, enabling models to dynamically interleave complex reasoning with on-demand tool usage (including search APIs and Python interpreters).\n To support this research, we introduce two new datasets: TAPO-easy-60K and TAPO-hard-18K, specifically designed to train and evaluate both fact-based reasoning and mathematical calculation capabilities. Our experiments on Qwen2.5-3B and Qwen2.5-7B models demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, with both models achieving state-of-the-art performance on tasks requiring external knowledge and mathematical computation among methods with comparable parameters. Notably, TAPO achieves more efficient tool utilization than baseline methods while preventing excessive calls caused by reward hacking. These results highlight the significant potential of combining advanced reasoning with tool usage to enhance model performance in knowledge-intensive and computationally demanding tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06471","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06471","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06471","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoxiong","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Shaoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörg","familyName":"Tiedemann","name":"Tiedemann, Jörg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Scaling of Reasoning Models for Machine Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:09:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T22:32:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:57:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling (TTS) has enhanced the performance of Reasoning Models (RMs) on various tasks such as math and coding, yet its efficacy in machine translation (MT) remains underexplored. This paper investigates whether increased inference-time computation improves translation quality. We evaluate 12 RMs across a diverse suite of MT benchmarks spanning multiple domains, examining three scenarios: direct translation, forced-reasoning extrapolation, and post-editing. Our findings show that for general-purpose RMs, TTS provides limited and inconsistent benefits for direct translation, with performance quickly plateauing. However, the effectiveness of TTS is unlocked by domain-specific fine-tuning, which aligns a model's reasoning process with task requirements, leading to consistent improvements up to an optimal, self-determined reasoning depth. We also find that forcing a model to reason beyond its natural stopping point consistently degrades translation quality. In contrast, TTS proves highly effective in a post-editing context, reliably turning self-correction into a beneficial process. These results indicate that the value of inference-time computation in MT lies not in enhancing single-pass translation with general models, but in targeted applications like multi-step, self-correction workflows and in conjunction with task-specialized models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06471","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07300","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07300","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07300","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fandong","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Songming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think Natively: Unlocking Multilingual Reasoning with Consistency-Enhanced Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:55:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T09:32:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:38:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T04:45:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) have achieved remarkable performance on complex reasoning tasks by adopting the ``think-then-answer'' paradigm, which enhances both accuracy and interpretability. However, current LRMs exhibit two critical limitations when processing non-English languages: (1) They often struggle to maintain input-output language consistency; (2) They generally perform poorly with wrong reasoning paths and lower answer accuracy compared to English. These limitations significantly compromise the interpretability of reasoning processes and degrade the user experience for non-English speakers, hindering the global deployment of LRMs. To address these limitations, we propose M-Thinker, which is trained by the GRPO algorithm that involves a Language Consistency (LC) reward and a novel Cross-lingual Thinking Alignment (CTA) reward. Specifically, the LC reward defines a strict constraint on the language consistency between the input, thought, and answer. Besides, the CTA reward compares the model's non-English reasoning paths with its English reasoning path to transfer its own reasoning capability from English to non-English languages. Through an iterative RL procedure, our M-Thinker-1.5B/4B/7B models not only achieve nearly 100% language consistency and superior performance on two multilingual benchmarks (MMATH and PolyMath), but also exhibit excellent generalization on out-of-domain languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 14 tables, 4 figures. Code is available at: https://github.com/XZhang00/M-Thinker"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07300","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T03:10:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Hippocampus Networks for Efficient Long-Context Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T07:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-sequence modeling faces a fundamental trade-off between the efficiency of compressive fixed-size memory in RNN-like models and the fidelity of lossless growing memory in attention-based Transformers. Inspired by the Multi-Store Model in cognitive science, we introduce a memory framework of artificial neural networks. Our method maintains a sliding window of the Transformer's KV cache as lossless short-term memory, while a learnable module termed Artificial Hippocampus Network (AHN) recurrently compresses out-of-window information into a fixed-size compact long-term memory. To validate this framework, we instantiate AHNs using modern RNN-like architectures, including Mamba2, DeltaNet, and GatedDeltaNet to augment open-weight LLMs. We also propose an efficient self-distillation training method where the base model's all parameters are frozen and only the parameters from AHNs are optimized. For inference, our method sets a default large sliding window size of 32k for attention, and AHNs activate only when the sequence length exceeds the 32k window, addressing the quadratic-complexity issue of attention that emerges at that scale. Extensive experiments on long-context benchmarks LV-Eval and InfiniteBench demonstrate that AHN-augmented models consistently outperform sliding window baselines and achieve performance comparable or even superior to full-attention models, while substantially reducing computational and memory requirements. For instance, augmenting the Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct with AHNs reduces inference FLOPs by 40.5% and memory cache by 74.0%, while improving its average score on LV-Eval (128k sequence length) from 4.41 to 5.88. Code is available at: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengpei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Chengpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingying","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Approximate Gaussian Mapping for Generative Image Steganography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:53:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T13:44:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ordinary differential equation (ODE)-based diffusion models enable deterministic image synthesis, establishing a reversible mapping suitable for generative steganography. While prevailing methods strictly adhere to a standard normal prior, empirical evidence indicates that controlled deviations from this distribution reduce numerical inversion errors without compromising perceptual quality. Leveraging this observation, the Approximate Gaussian Mapping (AGM) is proposed as a linear transformation strategy that embeds secrets by modulating noise scale and variance. To balance retrieval numerical consistence and security, a two-stage decoupled optimization strategy is introduced to minimize the Kullback-Leibler divergence subject to target bit accuracy constraints. Beyond the proposed method, we conduct a mechanistic analysis of the divergent behaviors between pixel-space and latent-space architectures. The experimental results reveal that the VAE encoder enhances robustness by filtering external perturbations, whereas the structural regularization of the VAE decoder and the semantic variance introduced by text prompts jointly mask embedding artifacts to improve security. Experimental results confirm that pixel-space mplementations maximize embedding capacity for lossless channels, while latent-space approaches offer superior robustness and security suitable for adversarial environments"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06349","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06349","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moein E.","familyName":"Samadi","name":"Samadi, Moein E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Schuppert","name":"Schuppert, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible Swarm Learning May Outpace Foundation Models in Essential Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:10:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T11:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:45:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have rapidly advanced AI, raising the question of whether their decisions will ultimately surpass human strategies in real-world domains. The exponential, and possibly super-exponential, pace of AI development makes such analysis elusive. Nevertheless, many application areas that matter for daily life and society show only modest gains so far; a prominent case is diagnosing and treating dynamically evolving disease in intensive care.\n The common challenge is adapting complex systems to dynamic environments. Effective strategies must optimize outcomes in systems composed of strongly interacting functions while avoiding shared side effects; this requires reliable, self-adaptive modeling. These tasks align with building digital twins of highly complex systems whose mechanisms are not fully or quantitatively understood. It is therefore essential to develop methods for self-adapting AI models with minimal data and limited mechanistic knowledge. As this challenge extends beyond medicine, AI should demonstrate clear superiority in these settings before assuming broader decision-making roles.\n We identify the curse of dimensionality as a fundamental barrier to efficient self-adaptation and argue that monolithic foundation models face conceptual limits in overcoming it. As an alternative, we propose a decentralized architecture of interacting small agent networks (SANs). We focus on agents representing the specialized substructure of the system, where each agent covers only a subset of the full system functions. Drawing on mathematical results on the learning behavior of SANs and evidence from existing applications, we argue that swarm-learning in diverse swarms can enable self-adaptive SANs to deliver superior decision-making in dynamic environments compared with monolithic foundation models, though at the cost of reduced reproducibility in detail."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06349","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ieva","familyName":"Bagdonaviciute","name":"Bagdonaviciute, Ieva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhav","familyName":"Vineet","name":"Vineet, Vibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics Knowledge in Frontier Models: A Diagnostic Study of Failure Modes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T21:33:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:11:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved impressive progress, it remains difficult to determine why they succeed or fail on complex reasoning tasks. Traditional benchmarks evaluate what models can answer correctly, not why they succeed or fail. In this work, we perform a failure-mode analysis of six frontier VLMs on three physics-based benchmarks - Physion, Physion++, and CLEVRER - by introducing custom subtests (for Physion and Physion++) and an integration of existing benchmark categories (for CLEVRER) to factor benchmark performance into distinct, testable capabilities. These subtests isolate perception (object, color, and occlusion recognition) and physics understanding (motion prediction and spatial reasoning), enabling us to test whether models attend to the correct entities and dynamics underlying their answers. Counterintuitively, subtest mastery correlates only weakly with benchmark accuracy: models often answer correctly without grounding in perception or physics. This suggests that current VLMs sometimes achieve benchmark scores for the wrong reasons, underscoring the need for diagnostics that expose hidden failure modes beyond aggregate metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures. Preprint. v2: Updated experiments and diagnostics; formatting fixes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:56Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ori","familyName":"Nizan","name":"Nizan, Ori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Shrout","name":"Shrout, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayellet","familyName":"Tal","name":"Tal, Ayellet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Concept Retrieval -- What and How?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:26:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:27:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T12:22:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:01:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T09:09:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:31:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A concept may reflect either a concrete or abstract idea. Given an input image, this paper seeks to retrieve other images that share its central concepts, capturing aspects of the underlying narrative. This goes beyond conventional retrieval or clustering methods, which emphasize visual or semantic similarity. We formally define the problem, outline key requirements, and introduce appropriate evaluation metrics. We propose a novel approach grounded in two key observations: (1) While each neighbor in the embedding space typically shares at least one concept with the query, not all neighbors necessarily share the same concept with one another. (2) Modeling this neighborhood with a bimodal Gaussian distribution uncovers meaningful structure that facilitates concept identification. Qualitative, quantitative, and human evaluations confirm the effectiveness of our approach. See the package on PyPI: https://pypi.org/project/coret/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:52Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-17T03:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06557","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06557","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06557","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milad","familyName":"Aghajohari","name":"Aghajohari, Milad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamran","familyName":"Chitsaz","name":"Chitsaz, Kamran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Kazemnejad","name":"Kazemnejad, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Sordoni","name":"Sordoni, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Courville","name":"Courville, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siva","familyName":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Siva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Markovian Thinker: Architecture-Agnostic Linear Scaling of Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-10T03:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T02:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has recently become a strong recipe for training reasoning LLMs that produce long chains of thought (LongCoT). Yet the standard RL \"thinking environment\", where the state is the prompt plus all prior reasoning tokens, makes the state unbounded and forces attention-based policies to pay quadratic compute as thoughts lengthen. We revisit the environment itself. We propose Markovian Thinking, a paradigm in which the policy advances reasoning while conditioning on a constant-size state, decoupling thinking length from context size. As an immediate consequence this yields linear compute with constant memory. We instantiate this idea with Delethink, an RL environment that structures reasoning into fixed-size chunks. Within each chunk, the model thinks as usual; at the boundary, the environment resets the context and reinitializes the prompt with a short carryover. Through RL, the policy learns to write a textual state near the end of each chunk sufficient for seamless continuation of reasoning after reset. Trained in this environment, an R1-Distill 1.5B model reasons in 8K-token chunks yet thinks up to 24K tokens, matching or surpassing LongCoT-RL trained with a 24K budget. With test-time scaling, Delethink continues to improve where LongCoT plateaus. The effect of linear compute is substantial: we empirically estimate at 96K average thinking length LongCoT-RL costs 27 H100-months vs. 7 for Delethink. Analysis at RL initialization shows off-the-shelf reasoning models (1.5B-120B) often sample Markovian traces zero-shot across diverse benchmarks, providing positive samples that make RL effective at scale. Our results show that redesigning the thinking environment is a powerful lever: it enables very long reasoning without quadratic overhead and opens a path toward efficient, scalable reasoning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06557","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:11:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07110","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07110","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07110","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tran","familyName":"Van Duy","name":"Van Duy, Tran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan Hai","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Tuan Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vu Trung Duong","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Vu Trung Duong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoai Luan","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hoai Luan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuhiko","familyName":"Nakashima","name":"Nakashima, Yasuhiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HPQEA: A Scalable and High-Performance Quantum Emulator with High-Bandwidth Memory for Diverse Algorithms Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:01:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:53:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T08:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:30:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, there has been a growing interest in the development of quantum emulation. However, existing studies often struggle to achieve broad applicability, high performance, and efficient resource and memory utilization. To address these challenges, we provide HPQEA, a quantum emulator based on the state-vector emulation approach. HPQEA includes three main features: a high-performance computing core, an optimized controlled-NOT gate computation strategy, and effective utilization of high-bandwidth memory. Verification and evaluation on the Alveo U280 board show that HPQEA can emulate quantum circuits with up to 30 qubits while maintaining high fidelity and low mean square error. It outperforms comparable FPGA-based systems by producing faster execution, supporting a wider range of algorithms, and requiring low hardware resources. Furthermore, it exceeds the Nvidia A100 in normalized gate speed for systems with up to 20 qubits. These results demonstrate the scalability and efficiency of HPQEA as a platform for emulating quantum algorithms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted at The Thirteenth International Symposium on Computing and Networking (CANDAR2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07110","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:07Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zecheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baibei","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Baibei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quantong","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Quantong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongRM: Revealing and Unlocking the Context Boundary of Reward Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:48:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T05:29:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:24:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reward model (RM) plays a pivotal role in aligning large language model (LLM) with human preferences. As real-world applications increasingly involve long history trajectories, e.g., LLM agent, it becomes indispensable to evaluate whether a model's responses are not only high-quality but also grounded in and consistent with the provided context. Yet, current RMs remain confined to short-context settings and primarily focus on response-level attributes (e.g., safety or helpfulness), while largely neglecting the critical dimension of long context-response consistency. In this work, we introduce Long-RewardBench, a benchmark specifically designed for long-context RM evaluation, featuring both Pairwise Comparison and Best-of-N tasks. Our preliminary study reveals that even state-of-the-art generative RMs exhibit significant fragility in long-context scenarios, failing to maintain context-aware preference judgments. Motivated by the analysis of failure patterns observed in model outputs, we propose a general multi-stage training strategy that effectively scales arbitrary models into robust Long-context RMs (LongRMs). Experiments show that our approach not only substantially improves performance on long-context evaluation but also preserves strong short-context capability. Notably, our 8B LongRM outperforms much larger 70B-scale baselines and matches the performance of the proprietary Gemini 2.5 Pro model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-05T03:12:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06658","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06658","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06658","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaman","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikang","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Zikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dana","familyName":"McKay","name":"McKay, Dana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damiano","familyName":"Spina","name":"Spina, Damiano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanne R.","familyName":"Trippas","name":"Trippas, Johanne R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can We Hide Machines in the Crowd? Quantifying Equivalence in LLM-in-the-loop Annotation Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T05:17:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T03:24:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:23:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769508","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many evaluations of large language models (LLMs) in text annotation focus primarily on the correctness of the output, typically comparing model-generated labels to human-annotated ``ground truth'' using standard performance metrics. In contrast, our study moves beyond effectiveness alone. We aim to explore how labeling decisions -- by both humans and LLMs -- can be statistically evaluated across individuals. Rather than treating LLMs purely as annotation systems, we approach LLMs as an alternative annotation mechanism that may be capable of mimicking the subjective judgments made by humans. To assess this, we develop a statistical evaluation method based on Krippendorff's $α$, paired bootstrapping, and the Two One-Sided t-Tests (TOST) equivalence test procedure. This evaluation method tests whether an LLM can blend into a group of human annotators without being distinguishable.\n We apply this approach to two datasets -- MovieLens 100K and PolitiFact -- and find that the LLM is statistically indistinguishable from a human annotator in the former ($p = 0.004$), but not in the latter ($p = 0.155$), highlighting task-dependent differences. It also enables early evaluation on a small sample of human data to inform whether LLMs are suitable for large-scale annotation in a given application."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at SIGIR-AP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06658","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhou","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Fangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianglin","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Jianglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuesong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuesong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ali","familyName":"Imran","name":"Imran, Muhammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Semantic Communication for UAV/UGV Cooperative Path Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:30:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T16:19:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T01:03:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective path planning is fundamental to the coordination of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs) systems, particularly in applications such as surveillance, navigation, and emergency response. Combining UAVs' broad field of view with UGVs' ground-level operational capability greatly improve the likelihood of successfully achieving task objectives such as locating victims, monitoring target areas, or navigating hazardous terrain. In complex environments, UAVs need to provide precise environmental perception information for UGVs to optimize their routing policy. However, due to severe interference and non-line-of-sight conditions, wireless communication is often unstable in such complex environments, making it difficult to support timely and accurate path planning for UAV-UGV coordination. To this end, this paper proposes a semantic communication (SemCom) framework to enhance UAV/UGV cooperative path planning under unreliable wireless conditions. Unlike traditional methods that transmit raw data, SemCom transmits only the key information for path planning, reducing transmission volume without sacrificing accuracy. The proposed framework is developed by defining key semantics for path planning and designing a transceiver for meeting the requirements of UAV-UGV cooperative path planning. Simulation results show that, compared to conventional SemCom transceivers, the proposed transceiver significantly reduces data transmission volume while maintaining path planning accuracy, thereby enhancing system collaboration efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-22T02:21:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Customer-R1: Personalized Simulation of Human Behaviors via RL-based LLM Agent in Online Shopping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:00:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T04:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:19:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Simulating step-wise human behavior with Large Language Models (LLMs) has become an emerging research direction, enabling applications in various practical domains. While prior methods, including prompting, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL), have shown promise in modeling step-wise behavior, they primarily learn a population-level policy without conditioning on a user's persona, yielding generic rather than personalized simulations. In this work, we pose a critical question: how can LLM agents better simulate personalized user behavior? We introduce Customer-R1, an RL-based method for personalized, step-wise user behavior simulation in online shopping environments. Our policy is conditioned on an explicit persona, and we optimize next-step rationale and action generation via action correctness reward signals. Experiments on the OPeRA dataset emonstrate that Customer-R1 not only significantly outperforms prompting and SFT-based baselines in next-action prediction tasks, but also better matches users' action distribution, indicating higher fidelity in personalized behavior simulation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06917","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06917","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06917","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng-Han","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Cheng-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chung-Ching","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chung-Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhendong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SHANKS: Simultaneous Hearing and Thinking for Spoken Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T07:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:24:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current large language models (LLMs) and spoken language models (SLMs) begin thinking and taking actions only after the user has finished their turn. This prevents the model from interacting during the user's turn and can lead to high response latency while it waits to think. Consequently, thinking after receiving the full input is not suitable for speech-to-speech interaction, where real-time, low-latency exchange is important. We address this by noting that humans naturally \"think while listening.\" In this paper, we propose SHANKS, a general inference framework that enables SLMs to generate unspoken chain-of-thought reasoning while listening to the user input. SHANKS streams the input speech in fixed-duration chunks and, as soon as a chunk is received, generates unspoken reasoning based on all previous speech and reasoning, while the user continues speaking. SHANKS uses this unspoken reasoning to decide whether to interrupt the user and to make tool calls to complete the task. We demonstrate that SHANKS enhances real-time user-SLM interaction in two scenarios: (1) when the user is presenting a step-by-step solution to a math problem, SHANKS can listen, reason, and interrupt when the user makes a mistake, achieving 37.1% higher interruption accuracy than a baseline that interrupts without thinking; and (2) in a tool-augmented dialogue, SHANKS can complete 56.9% of the tool calls before the user finishes their turn. Overall, SHANKS moves toward models that keep thinking throughout the conversation, not only after a turn ends. Animated illustrations of Shanks can be found at https://d223302.github.io/SHANKS/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06917","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongbo","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zhongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qipeng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDAR: A Synergistic Diffusion-AutoRegression Paradigm for Scalable Sequence Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T20:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:38:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose SDAR, a Synergistic Diffusion-Autoregression paradigm that unifies the training efficiency of autoregressive models with the parallel inference capability of diffusion. Instead of costly end-to-end diffusion training, SDAR performs a lightweight paradigm conversion that transforms a well-trained autoregressive (AR) model into a blockwise diffusion model through brief, data-efficient adaptation. During inference, SDAR generates sequences autoregressively across blocks for global coherence while decoding all tokens within each block in parallel via a discrete diffusion process. Extensive experiments show that AR models remain substantially more compute-efficient than masked diffusion models, providing a strong foundation for adaptation. Building on this insight, SDAR achieves efficient AR-to-diffusion conversion with minimal cost, preserving AR-level performance while enabling parallel generation. Scaling studies across dense and Mixture-of-Experts architectures confirm that SDAR scales without compromise: larger models exhibit stronger robustness to block size and decoding thresholds, yielding greater speedups without accuracy loss. Beyond efficiency, SDAR demonstrates enhanced reasoning and domain adaptability. Our 30B MoE model surpasses its AR counterpart on challenging scientific reasoning benchmarks such as GPQA and ChemBench, and gains further improvements under test-time scaling methods like majority voting and pass@k. Together, these results establish SDAR as a practical paradigm that combines the strengths of autoregression and diffusion for scalable, high-throughput reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report. 40 pages, Inference speedup analysis added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07242","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07242","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07242","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leitian","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Leitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Kulikov","name":"Kulikov, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swarnadeep","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Swarnadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason E","familyName":"Weston","name":"Weston, Jason E","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Reinforcement: When Reward Is Sparse, It's Better to Be Dense"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:09:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:09:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T10:03:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:37:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training for reasoning of large language models (LLMs) increasingly relies on verifiable rewards: deterministic checkers that provide 0-1 correctness signals. While reliable, such binary feedback is brittle--many tasks admit partially correct or alternative answers that verifiers under-credit, and the resulting all-or-nothing supervision limits learning. Reward models offer richer, continuous feedback, which can serve as a complementary supervisory signal to verifiers. We introduce HERO (Hybrid Ensemble Reward Optimization), a reinforcement learning framework that integrates verifier signals with reward-model scores in a structured way. HERO employs stratified normalization to bound reward-model scores within verifier-defined groups, preserving correctness while refining quality distinctions, and variance-aware weighting to emphasize challenging prompts where dense signals matter most. Across diverse mathematical reasoning benchmarks, HERO consistently outperforms RM-only and verifier-only baselines, with strong gains on both verifiable and hard-to-verify tasks. Our results show that hybrid reward design retains the stability of verifiers while leveraging the nuance of reward models to advance reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07242","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:02:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumeet Ramesh","familyName":"Motwani","name":"Motwani, Sumeet Ramesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alesia","familyName":"Ivanova","name":"Ivanova, Alesia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riashat","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Riashat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shital","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Shital","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian Schroeder","familyName":"de Witt","name":"de Witt, Christian Schroeder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"London","name":"London, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T17:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T01:07:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models excel at short-horizon reasoning tasks, but performance drops as reasoning horizon lengths increase. Existing approaches to combat this rely on inference-time scaffolding or costly step-level supervision, neither of which scales easily. In this work, we introduce a scalable method to bootstrap long-horizon reasoning capabilities using only existing, abundant short-horizon data. Our approach synthetically composes simple problems into complex, multi-step dependency chains of arbitrary length. We train models on this data using outcome-only rewards under a curriculum that automatically increases in complexity, allowing RL training to be scaled much further without saturating. Empirically, our method generalizes remarkably well: curriculum training on composed 6th-grade level math problems (GSM8K) boosts accuracy on longer, competition-level benchmarks (GSM-Symbolic, MATH-500, AIME) by up to 2.06x. It also transfers significantly to diverse out-of-distribution ReasoningGym domains and long-context benchmarks, indicating broader generalization. Importantly, our long-horizon improvements are significantly higher than baselines even at high pass@k, showing that models can learn new reasoning paths under RL. Theoretically, we show that curriculum RL with outcome rewards achieves an exponential improvement in sample complexity over full-horizon training, providing training signal comparable to dense supervision. h1 therefore introduces an efficient path towards scaling RL for long-horizon problems using only existing data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, 31 pages, 8 figures, long-horizon reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:55Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russell","familyName":"Beale","name":"Beale, Russell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Am I Productive? Exploring the Experience of Remote Workers with Task Management Tools"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:41:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:56:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3750069.3750107","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the world continues to change, more and more knowledge workers are embracing remote work. Yet this comes with its challenges for their productivity, and while many Task Management applications promise to improve the productivity of remote workers, it remains unclear how effective they are. Based on existing frameworks, this study investigated the productivity needs and challenges of remote knowledge workers and how they use Task Management tools. The research was conducted through a 2-week long, mixed-methods diary study and semi-structured interview. Perceptions of productivity, task management tool use and productivity challenges were observed. The findings show that using a digital Task Management application made no significant difference to using pen and paper for improving perceived productivity of remote workers and discuss the need for better personalization of Task Management applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:07Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akira","familyName":"Kamimaki","name":"Kamimaki, Akira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhei","familyName":"Sekiguchi","name":"Sekiguchi, Yuhei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Ito","name":"Ito, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taichi","familyName":"Fujiwara","name":"Fujiwara, Taichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshiharu","familyName":"Makino","name":"Makino, Toshiharu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiromitsu","familyName":"Kato","name":"Kato, Hiromitsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideo","familyName":"Kosaka","name":"Kosaka, Hideo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modular interface for efficient optical readout of diamond quantum memory at cryogenic temperatures via single-mode optical fibers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:01:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T05:06:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient quantum devices across various physical systems have been rapidly developed for entanglement-based quantum repeaters and spin-photon conversion; however, far less attention has been paid to standardizing platforms through quantum memory optical interfaces. We present a modular interface for color centers in diamond that is structurally isolated from device-and temperature-related variation. Despite a more than 100-fold reduction in confocal volume, we achieve highly efficient photon collection through single-mode optical fibers, including zero-phonon line spectroscopy, at both room and cryogenic temperatures. These results establish a standardized minimal NV-center-based platform and pave the way for construction of scalable quantum infrastructure."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:23:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baraq","familyName":"Lipshitz","name":"Lipshitz, Baraq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Melone","name":"Melone, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charalampos","familyName":"Maraziaris","name":"Maraziaris, Charalampos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammed","familyName":"Bilal","name":"Bilal, Muhammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating Sparse Ternary GEMM for Quantized ML on Apple Silicon"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:42:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T15:54:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Ternary General Matrix-Matrix Multiplication (GEMM) remains under-optimized in existing libraries for Apple Silicon CPUs. We present a Sparse Ternary GEMM kernel optimized specifically for Apple's M-series processors. We propose a set of architecture-aware optimizations, including a novel blocked and interleaved sparse data format to improve memory locality, strategies to increase Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP), and NEON-based Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) vectorization to exploit data-level parallelism. Our scalar implementation achieves up to a 5.98x performance increase over a traditional Ternary Compressed Sparse Column (TCSC) baseline for large matrices with 50% ternary nonzero values (sparsity), reaching up to a 50.2% of the processor's theoretical peak performance, and remains stable across varying sparsity levels. Our vectorized implementation delivers up to a 5.59x performance increase for large matrices with 25% sparsity, and remains stable across varying sparsity levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:23:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pulkit","familyName":"Rustagi","name":"Rustagi, Pulkit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle Hollins","familyName":"Wray","name":"Wray, Kyle Hollins","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandhya","familyName":"Saisubramanian","name":"Saisubramanian, Sandhya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Objective Multi-Agent Path Finding with Lexicographic Cost Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:40:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:02:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T20:46:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:15:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many real-world scenarios require multiple agents to coordinate in shared environments, while balancing trade-offs between multiple, potentially competing objectives. Current multi-objective multi-agent path finding (MO-MAPF) algorithms typically produce conflict-free plans by computing Pareto frontiers. They do not explicitly optimize for user-defined preferences, even when the preferences are available, and scale poorly with the number of objectives. We propose a lexicographic framework for modeling MO-MAPF, along with an algorithm \\textit{Lexicographic Conflict-Based Search} (LCBS) that directly computes a single solution aligned with a lexicographic preference over objectives. LCBS integrates a priority-aware low-level $A^*$ search with conflict-based search, avoiding Pareto frontier construction and enabling efficient planning guided by preference over objectives. We provide insights into optimality and scalability, and empirically demonstrate that LCBS computes optimal solutions while scaling to instances with up to ten objectives -- far beyond the limits of existing MO-MAPF methods. Evaluations on standard and randomized MAPF benchmarks show consistently higher success rates against state-of-the-art baselines, especially with increasing number of objectives."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:24:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06343","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06343","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06343","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fikret Mert","familyName":"Gultekin","name":"Gultekin, Fikret Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Lilja","name":"Lilja, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranim","familyName":"Khojah","name":"Khojah, Ranim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebekka","familyName":"Wohlrab","name":"Wohlrab, Rebekka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marvin","familyName":"Damschen","name":"Damschen, Marvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mazen","familyName":"Mohamad","name":"Mohamad, Mazen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Large Language Models for Cybersecurity Risk Assessment -- A Case from Forestry Cyber-Physical Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T19:52:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:46:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In safety-critical software systems, cybersecurity activities become essential, with risk assessment being one of the most critical. In many software teams, cybersecurity experts are either entirely absent or represented by only a small number of specialists. As a result, the workload for these experts becomes high, and software engineers would need to conduct cybersecurity activities themselves. This creates a need for a tool to support cybersecurity experts and engineers in evaluating vulnerabilities and threats during the risk assessment process. This paper explores the potential of leveraging locally hosted large language models (LLMs) with retrieval-augmented generation to support cybersecurity risk assessment in the forestry domain while complying with data protection and privacy requirements that limit external data sharing. We performed a design science study involving 12 experts in interviews, interactive sessions, and a survey within a large-scale project. The results demonstrate that LLMs can assist cybersecurity experts by generating initial risk assessments, identifying threats, and providing redundancy checks. The results also highlight the necessity for human oversight to ensure accuracy and compliance. Despite trust concerns, experts were willing to utilize LLMs in specific evaluation and assistance roles, rather than solely relying on their generative capabilities. This study provides insights that encourage the use of LLM-based agents to support the risk assessment process of cyber-physical systems in safety-critical domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Autonomous Agents in Software Engineering (AgenticSE) Workshop, co-located with ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06343","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:24:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MohammadHossein","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, MohammadHossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Vacareanu","name":"Vacareanu, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clinton","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Clinton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Afra Feyza","familyName":"Akyürek","name":"Akyürek, Afra Feyza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online Rubrics Elicitation from Pairwise Comparisons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:44:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:26:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rubrics provide a flexible way to train LLMs on open-ended long-form answers where verifiable rewards are not applicable and human preferences provide coarse signals. Prior work shows that reinforcement learning with rubric-based rewards leads to consistent gains in LLM post-training. Most existing approaches rely on rubrics that remain static over the course of training. Such static rubrics, however, are vulnerable to reward-hacking type behaviors and fail to capture emergent desiderata that arise during training. We introduce Online Rubrics Elicitation (OnlineRubrics), a method that dynamically curates evaluation criteria in an online manner through pairwise comparisons of responses from current and reference policies. This online process enables continuous identification and mitigation of errors as training proceeds. Empirically, this approach yields consistent improvements of up to 8% over training exclusively with static rubrics across AlpacaEval, GPQA, ArenaHard as well as the validation sets of expert questions and rubrics. We qualitatively analyze the elicited criteria and identify prominent themes such as transparency, practicality, organization, and reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:14Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:18:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06955","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06955","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06955","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masih","familyName":"Aminbeidokhti","name":"Aminbeidokhti, Masih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heitor Rapela","familyName":"Medeiros","name":"Medeiros, Heitor Rapela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srikanth","familyName":"Muralidharan","name":"Muralidharan, Srikanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High-Rate Mixout: Revisiting Mixout for Robust Domain Generalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:37:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T23:50:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:13:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensembling fine-tuned models initialized from powerful pre-trained weights is a common strategy to improve robustness under distribution shifts, but it comes with substantial computational costs due to the need to train and store multiple models. Dropout offers a lightweight alternative by simulating ensembles through random neuron deactivation; however, when applied to pre-trained models, it tends to over-regularize and disrupt critical representations necessary for generalization. In this work, we investigate Mixout, a stochastic regularization technique that provides an alternative to Dropout for domain generalization. Rather than deactivating neurons, Mixout mitigates overfitting by probabilistically swapping a subset of fine-tuned weights with their pre-trained counterparts during training, thereby maintaining a balance between adaptation and retention of prior knowledge. Our study reveals that achieving strong performance with Mixout on domain generalization benchmarks requires a notably high masking probability of 0.9 for ViTs and 0.8 for ResNets. While this may seem like a simple adjustment, it yields two key advantages for domain generalization: (1) higher masking rates more strongly penalize deviations from the pre-trained parameters, promoting better generalization to unseen domains; and (2) high-rate masking substantially reduces computational overhead, cutting gradient computation by up to 45% and gradient memory usage by up to 90%. Experiments across five domain generalization benchmarks, PACS, VLCS, OfficeHome, TerraIncognita, and DomainNet, using ResNet and ViT architectures, show that our approach, High-rate Mixout, achieves out-of-domain accuracy comparable to ensemble-based methods while significantly reducing training costs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WACV 2026: Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06955","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:23Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:18:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":201,"totalPages":3,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+retrieval+OR+RAG+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+memory%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+retrieval+OR+RAG+OR+reasoning+OR+planning+OR+memory%29"}}